const NES_EMU_APU_HTML = "<section class=\"gbemu-hero\"><div class=\"gbemu-hero-num\"><b>APU &amp; expansion audio</b><span>build specification</span></div><div class=\"gbemu-hero-txt\"><h3>APU &amp; expansion audio: how to build it, exactly as our core runs it</h3><p>The audio unit: five channels, the frame counter, the exact mixer, the output filters and sample rate; then every expansion sound chip and how it joins the mix. Every statement says how it is known. This is one area of the build specification; the index lists all eight.</p></div></section>\n<nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>In this area</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">The audio unit inside the 2A03</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">Expansion audio: VRC6, VRC7, Namco 163, Sunsoft 5B, MMC5 and the Disk System</a></nav>\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu\">The audio unit inside the 2A03</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s1\">1. Conventions used in this chapter</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s2\">2. Order of work inside one audio tick</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s3\">3. The register map, <code>$4000</code>-<code>$4017</code></a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s4\">4. The envelope (pulse 1, pulse 2, noise)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s5\">5. The length counter (pulse 1, pulse 2, triangle, noise)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s6\">6. The pulse channels</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s7\">7. The triangle</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s8\">8. The noise channel</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s9\">9. The DMC (delta-modulation channel)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s10\">10. The frame counter</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s11\">11. Power-on and reset</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s12\">12. The mixer</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s13\">13. The output stage: averaging and the console's filters</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s14\">14. The knobs</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s15\">15. Registered choices, collected</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu-s16\">16. Glossary</a></nav>\n<p>The audio unit has five channels (two pulse, one triangle, one noise, one delta-modulation channel, the DMC), a frame counter that is really a timer with an interrupt, a non-linear mixer, the console's output filters, and a sample queue the host drains. One part of it reaches into the CPU's schedule: the DMC asks for bytes, and the direct memory access unit that fetches them is specified in <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">the CPU chapter</a>. This chapter states the request side (when a byte is asked for) and the knobs that set the cost; <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a> states how the stolen cycles run. The terms used here and in <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a> are defined once, in section 16.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> <b>Nothing in this unit is justified by listening.</b> A wrong constant in audio still plays music, so every timing quantity here is either pinned by a test cartridge that reports its own verdict, carried as a runtime knob that was swept from both sides, or stated as not established on hardware, with the core's default. A wrong length table entry, a frame counter one cycle off, or missing cycle theft are all audible only against a recording, which is not a measurement.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s1\">1. Conventions used in this chapter</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>CPU cycle numbering.</b> <code>cycles</code> is the CPU's cycle counter. It is set to 0 at power-on (not at reset) and incremented <b>at the start</b> of every CPU cycle, before anything else in that cycle happens. \"Cycle N\" means the cycle during which the counter reads N. The seven cycles of the reset sequence are cycles 1-7 after power-on; the first instruction's opcode fetch is cycle 8 (stated in <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>).</li><li><b>Parity</b> means <code>cycles</code> modulo 2, using this absolute numbering. Several facts below are \"on odd cycles\" or \"on even cycles\"; they are all stated against this count.</li><li><b>The audio tick.</b> Every CPU cycle does these steps, in this order:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the picture unit's dots for that cycle;</li><li>the cartridge's own clock;</li><li><b>one audio tick</b> (section 2);</li><li>the cycle's bus access (a read or write by the CPU);</li><li>the end of the cycle (the put/get transition of section 3.6), then the polling of the interrupt lines.</li></ol>\n<p>So a register write performed during cycle N lands <b>after</b> cycle N's tick; the first tick that sees it is cycle N+1's. A <code>$4015</code> read during cycle N sees everything cycle N's tick did. The 2005 frame-counter suite (<code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30</code>) and <code>apu_test</code> confirm the ordering; the access-after-tick order is the CPU chapter's.</p></li><li><b>Units.</b> Every period below is in <b>CPU cycles</b>, never in \"APU cycles\".</li><li><b>Region.</b> \"PAL\" below means region 1. <b>Dendy (region 2) uses the NTSC tables and the NTSC filter rate everywhere in this unit</b>: every region test in the unit is \"is it PAL\", so Dendy takes the NTSC branch (taken from another emulator's source: \"the Dendy audio unit works with NTSC timings\").</li></ul>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Published schedules use APU cycles and contain half cycles (3728.5, 14914.5); counting in CPU cycles makes every entry an integer and removes a rounding decision. The Dendy clone was built so NTSC software and its pitches run unmodified.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s2\">2. Order of work inside one audio tick</h4>\n<p>The tick does these steps, in this order, every CPU cycle. If the knob <code>enabled</code> (<code>APU_P_ENABLED</code> = 7) is 0 the tick does nothing at all (no timers, no frame counter, no samples emitted) and register writes to the unit are ignored (<code>$4015</code> reads still work).</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Apply delayed halt flags</b> for pulse 1, pulse 2, triangle, noise (section 5.2).</li><li><b>Frame counter</b> (section 10): count down an in-flight <code>$4017</code> write, or advance the step counter and perform any step that matches.</li><li><b>DMC start delay</b>: if a <code>$4015</code> enable's first fetch request is in flight, count it down; when it reaches 0, make the request (section 9.4).</li><li><b>Pulse timers</b>, only on cycles whose parity equals <code>NES_APU_PULSE_PARITY</code> (section 6.2).</li><li><b>Noise timer</b> (every cycle by default, section 8).</li><li><b>Triangle timer</b> (every cycle, section 7).</li><li><b>DMC timer</b> (every cycle, section 9).</li><li><b>Mix</b> this cycle's channel outputs, add expansion audio, accumulate; if the accumulator holds a full sample period, filter and emit one sample (sections 12-14).</li></ol>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s3\">3. The register map, <code>$4000</code>-<code>$4017</code></h4>\n<p>All registers in <code>$4000</code>-<code>$4013</code>, <code>$4015</code> (write) and <code>$4017</code> (write) belong to the unit. <code>$4014</code> (sprite DMA) and <code>$4016</code> (controller strobe) are written elsewhere. <b><code>$4017</code> is two registers</b>: written, it is the frame counter; read, it is controller port 2. <b><code>$4015</code> is the only readable audio register</b>; every other address in <code>$4000</code>-<code>$4014</code> and <code>$4018</code>-<code>$401F</code> reads as open bus (the last value on the data bus), not as zero and not as the value written. Writes to <code>$4009</code>, <code>$400D</code> and <code>$4018</code>-<code>$401F</code> do nothing (confirmed by <code>apu_test/1-len_ctr</code>, <code>apu_test/7-dmc_basics</code>, <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30</code>).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.1 Pulse 1 (<code>$4000</code>-<code>$4003</code>) and pulse 2 (<code>$4004</code>-<code>$4007</code>)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>register</th><th>bits</th><th>effect</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4000</code> / <code>$4004</code></td><td>7-6</td><td>duty pattern index (section 6.1)</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5</td><td><b>one bit, two meanings</b>: envelope loop (takes effect at once) AND length-counter halt (takes effect after the one-clock delay, section 5.2)</td><td>test: <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30</code> <code>10.len_halt_timing</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4</td><td>constant-volume flag: 1 = output the bits 3-0 value directly; 0 = output the envelope's decay level</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>envelope period / constant volume</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4001</code> / <code>$4005</code></td><td>7</td><td>sweep enable</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6-4</td><td>sweep divider period</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3</td><td>sweep negate</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2-0</td><td>sweep shift count</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(any write)</td><td>sets the sweep reload flag</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4002</code> / <code>$4006</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>timer period, low 8 bits</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4003</code> / <code>$4007</code></td><td>2-0</td><td>timer period, high 3 bits (period is 11 bits)</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>7-3</td><td>length load index into the length table (section 5.1); ignored when the channel is disabled in <code>$4015</code>; subject to the reload block (section 5.3)</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/2-len_table</code>, <code>11.len_reload_timing</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(any write)</td><td>sequencer step reset to 0; envelope start flag set. <b>The timer is NOT reloaded</b>: a rapid pitch change does not click</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.2 Triangle (<code>$4008</code>-<code>$400B</code>)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>register</th><th>bits</th><th>effect</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4008</code></td><td>7</td><td>linear-counter control (takes effect at once) AND length-counter halt (after the one-clock delay, section 5.2)</td><td>test: <code>10.len_halt_timing</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6-0</td><td>linear-counter reload value</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4009</code></td><td>(none)</td><td>unused; writes ignored</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$400A</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>timer period, low 8 bits</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$400B</code></td><td>2-0</td><td>timer period, high 3 bits</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>7-3</td><td>length load (as <code>$4003</code>)</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/2-len_table</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(any write)</td><td>sets the linear-counter reload flag. The sequencer is <b>not</b> reset</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.3 Noise (<code>$400C</code>-<code>$400F</code>)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>register</th><th>bits</th><th>effect</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$400C</code></td><td>5</td><td>envelope loop (at once) AND length halt (after the delay)</td><td>test: <code>10.len_halt_timing</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4</td><td>constant-volume flag</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>envelope period / constant volume</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$400D</code></td><td>(none)</td><td>unused; writes ignored</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$400E</code></td><td>7</td><td>mode: 1 = short (feedback from bit 6), 0 = long (feedback from bit 1)</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>period index. The stored period is the region's table entry <b>minus one</b>, chosen at the moment of the write by the machine's region (section 8)</td><td>hardware measurement</td></tr><tr><td><code>$400F</code></td><td>7-3</td><td>length load (as <code>$4003</code>)</td><td>confirmed by test</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(any write)</td><td>envelope start flag set</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.4 The DMC (<code>$4010</code>-<code>$4013</code>)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>register</th><th>bits</th><th>effect</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4010</code></td><td>7</td><td>interrupt enable. <b>Writing 0 here also clears the DMC interrupt flag</b> and releases its interrupt line</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/7-dmc_basics</code> item 12</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6</td><td>loop</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>rate index into the rate table (section 9.1). Takes effect at the next timer reload, not at once</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/8-dmc_rates</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>$4011</code></td><td>6-0</td><td>written straight into the 7-bit output level (bit 7 ignored). This is how a program plays PCM (raw levels written by the CPU) without the DMC's stream at all</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4012</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>stream start address = <code>$C000</code> + value × 64</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4013</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>stream length = value × 16 + 1 bytes. <b>A value of 0 gives a one-byte stream</b>, not silence</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/7-dmc_basics</code> item 18</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.5 <code>$4015</code> written: channel enables</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>pulse 1 enable; writing 0 forces its length counter to 0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>pulse 2 enable; 0 forces length 0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>triangle enable; 0 forces length 0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>noise enable; 0 forces length 0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>DMC: 0 = bytes remaining forced to 0, any pending fetch request cancelled, any in-flight start delay cancelled (the byte already in the buffer still plays out). 1 = if bytes remaining is 0, restart the stream (address and length reloaded from <code>$4012</code>/<code>$4013</code>) and schedule the first fetch request (section 9.4); if bytes remain, nothing changes</td></tr><tr><td>7-5</td><td>ignored</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Every <code>$4015</code> write clears the DMC interrupt flag and NOT the frame interrupt flag</b> (confirmed by <code>apu_test/7-dmc_basics</code> items 11 and 19, <code>apu_test/1-len_ctr</code>).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.6 <code>$4015</code> read: status</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>pulse 1 length counter &gt; 0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>pulse 2 length counter &gt; 0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>triangle length counter &gt; 0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>noise length counter &gt; 0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>DMC bytes remaining &gt; 0</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><b>open bus</b>: bit 5 of the 2A03's <b>internal</b> data latch (<a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a> §3.2). Nothing drives it. The read itself goes to the internal latch only and leaves the external one alone</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>frame interrupt flag</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>DMC interrupt flag</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>Reading <code>$4015</code> clears the frame interrupt flag, every time, with no special case, but not at once.</b> Switch <code>NES_R18_FRAME_IRQ_PHASE</code> = 1. The rules:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>The read composes the value from the table above first, so it returns the flag as it stands.</li><li>The read then records a <b>clear request</b>. It changes nothing else.</li><li>At the <b>next put→get transition</b> (the end of the next put cycle, section 16) the flag is cleared, the frame interrupt line is released and the request is dropped. That is the end of the read's own cycle if that cycle is a put, and the end of the following cycle if the read's cycle is a get. With the default <code>dma_align</code> = 2, puts are the even cycles and gets the odd ones (section 14).</li><li><b>A frame-flag set does not cancel a pending clear request.</b> The flag-set steps of section 10.2 run in the tick at the start of a cycle. A set in a cycle whose end is the transition sets the flag, and the clear at the end of that same cycle clears it again. A set in any later cycle sets the flag again and it stays set.</li><li>The read does not clear the DMC interrupt flag (confirmed by <code>07.irq_flag_timing</code> sub-tests 4 and 5).</li><li>Do <b>not</b> implement the published rule \"a read on the same cycle the flag is set returns 1 and does not clear it\" as a rule of its own. Rules 1-4 and the three set cycles of section 10.2 already produce what that rule describes; implementing it literally as well fails the last sub-test of <code>07.irq_flag_timing</code>.</li><li>With the switch at 0 the clear is immediate: the flag and its line drop during the read.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Worked example (defaults: gets odd, puts even; inhibit clear).</b> The flag-set steps fall on cycles 1001, 1002 and 1003.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>read during cycle</th><th>value of bit 6 returned</th><th>what happens next</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1001 (a get)</td><td>1 (set by 1001's tick)</td><td>end of 1001: get→put, nothing. 1002's tick sets the flag (already 1). End of 1002 (a put): cleared. 1003's tick sets it again; it stays 1</td></tr><tr><td>1002 (a put)</td><td>1</td><td>end of 1002: cleared. 1003's tick sets it again; it stays 1</td></tr><tr><td>1003 (a get)</td><td>1</td><td>end of 1003: nothing. 1004's tick sets nothing. A second read during 1004 still returns 1 (the double read of <code>SLO $4015,X</code>). End of 1004 (a put): cleared</td></tr><tr><td>1004 (a put)</td><td>1</td><td>end of 1004: cleared; nothing sets it again until the next frame</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The flag is an APU-side latch clocked on the APU phase, as measured on hardware; AccuracyCoin \"Frame Counter IRQ\" tests 6-7 confirm it: <code>SLO $4015,X</code> reads <code>$4015</code> twice on consecutive cycles, and the second read still sees bit 6 exactly when the first was on a get. Failure mode of clearing at once: a read on a get loses bit 6 one cycle early, and every program that uses the double read as a put/get detector reads \"put\" always. The published \"read on the set cycle\" rule is what the mechanism looks like from the program: the flag is set on three consecutive cycles (section 10.2), so a read on the middle one returns 1, clears it, and the next cycle's set restores it.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">3.7 <code>$4017</code> written: frame counter</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>bit</th><th>effect</th><th>when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7</td><td>mode: 0 = four-step, 1 = five-step</td><td>at the restart, after the write delay (section 10.3)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>interrupt inhibit. Writing 1 <b>clears the frame interrupt flag</b> and releases its line</td><td>at once, during the write</td></tr><tr><td>5-0</td><td>ignored (but the whole value is remembered for reset, section 11)</td><td>(not applicable)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s4\">4. The envelope (pulse 1, pulse 2, noise)</h4>\n<p>State: start flag, divider, decay level (0-15), period (<code>$4000</code> bits 3-0), constant flag, loop flag. Clocked on every <b>quarter-frame</b> step (section 10). On each clock:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the start flag is set: clear it, set decay to 15, set the divider to the period.</li><li>Otherwise, if the divider is non-zero: decrement it.</li><li>Otherwise: reload the divider with the period; then if decay is non-zero decrement it, else if the loop flag is set, set decay to 15.</li></ol>\n<p>The channel's volume is the period value itself when the constant flag is set, otherwise the decay level. <code>apu_test</code> and the 2005 suite confirm the envelope as a whole.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s5\">5. The length counter (pulse 1, pulse 2, triangle, noise)</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.1 The table</h5>\n<p>The length load index is bits 7-3 of <code>$4003</code>/<code>$4007</code>/<code>$400B</code>/<code>$400F</code>. <code>APU_LENGTH_TABLE</code>, 32 entries (each a count of half-frame clocks), in index order:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>index</th><th>values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>10, 254, 20, 2, 40, 4, 80, 6</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>160, 8, 60, 10, 14, 12, 26, 14</td></tr><tr><td>16-23</td><td>12, 16, 24, 18, 48, 20, 96, 22</td></tr><tr><td>24-31</td><td>192, 24, 72, 26, 16, 28, 32, 30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Odd indices 3, 5, 7 … 31 are the linear run 2, 4, 6 … 30 (fifteen values) and <b>index 1 is the exception, 254</b>; the even indices are note lengths at a nominal tempo. <code>apu_test/2-len_table</code> and <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30/02.len_table</code> confirm the table and print the correct value on failure.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Every entry is a plausible length, so a transcription error plays music rather than silence.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.2 Clocking and the delayed halt</h5>\n<p>The counter is clocked on every <b>half-frame</b> step: if a skip is pending (section 5.3), clear it and do nothing; otherwise, if the counter is non-zero and the halt flag is clear, decrement it. A counter at 0 stays at 0. A channel whose <code>$4015</code> enable bit is 0 ignores length loads entirely.</p>\n<p><b>The halt flag is delayed by one clock.</b> A write of the halt bit (<code>$4000</code>/<code>$4004</code> bit 5, <code>$4008</code> bit 7, <code>$400C</code> bit 5) during cycle N records the new value and N. At the start of each tick (step 1 of section 2) a pending value is applied <b>when the current cycle number is greater than N + 1</b>, i.e. at cycle N+2's tick, before that tick's frame counter step. So:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>a half-frame clock on the tick of cycle N+1 (the clock \"at the end of\" the write's cycle) still sees the <b>old</b> halt value;</li><li>a clock on cycle N+2 or later sees the new one.</li></ul>\n<p>The envelope-loop half of the same bit, and the triangle's linear-counter control, take effect immediately.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> That gives the four cases <code>10.len_halt_timing</code> tests: a halt written on the cycle before a length clock suppresses it, one written on the clock's cycle does not, and the same pair for un-halting. Without the delay two of four pass by coincidence (confirmed by <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30/10.len_halt_timing</code>).</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">5.3 The reload block</h5>\n<p>A length load written <b>simultaneously with a length clock</b> is ignored if the counter was non-zero, and honoured if it was zero; when honoured, the clock must not decrement the value just loaded (confirmed by <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30/11.len_reload_timing</code>, all four cases, NTSC and PAL).</p>\n<p>\"Simultaneous\" is decided <b>before</b> the clock, by asking \"will a half-frame clock land k ticks from now?\", where k is the knob <code>reload_offset</code> (<code>APU_P_RELOAD_OFFSET</code> = 6, default 1). The prediction:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If k is 0, the answer is no.</li><li>If a <code>$4017</code> write is in flight (its delay counter is non-zero): the answer is yes only when the delay counter equals k <b>and</b> the pending value's bit 7 is 1 (a five-step restart clocks everything); otherwise <b>no</b>, even if the running sequence will reach a half-frame step during the delay (section 10.3).</li><li>Otherwise: look up the step whose cycle equals the current step counter + k in the current mode's table; the answer is yes when that step carries the half-frame flag.</li></ol>\n<p>On a length load to an enabled channel:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>prediction</th><th>counter before the load</th><th>result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>yes</td><td>non-zero</td><td>the write is discarded (and the counter of blocked reloads, <code>nes_apu_reloads_blocked</code>, is incremented)</td></tr><tr><td>yes</td><td>zero</td><td>the counter is loaded and a one-shot <b>skip</b> is set, so the next length clock on that channel does nothing except clear the skip</td></tr><tr><td>no</td><td>any</td><td>the counter is loaded</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With k = 1 the clock in question is the one on the next tick, which is \"the end of the write's own cycle\".</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> A sweep of 0..3 confirms k = 1: 1 passes in both regions, 2 fails sub-test 2 (\"reload just before length clock should work normally\"), 0 and 3 fail sub-test 4 because both never block.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s6\">6. The pulse channels</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">6.1 The duty patterns</h5>\n<p><code>APU_DUTY</code>, four patterns of eight steps; the output is non-zero only on a step holding 1:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>duty (<code>$4000</code> bits 7-6)</th><th>steps 0-7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>duty 0 (12.5 %)</td><td>0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>duty 1 (25 %)</td><td>0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>duty 2 (50 %)</td><td>0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>duty 3 (25 % negated, NOT 75 %)</td><td>1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The channel has no waveform memory, only a three-bit step counter driving a four-to-one multiplexer of fixed patterns.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">6.2 The timer, and which cycles it runs on</h5>\n<p>Each pulse has an 11-bit period and a timer (a signed down-counter, 0 at power-on). The pulse timers run on <b>the APU clock, every second CPU cycle</b>: they are ticked only on cycles whose parity equals <code>NES_APU_PULSE_PARITY</code> (default 1 = <b>odd</b> cycles). On each such tick the timer is decremented; when it goes below 0 it is reloaded with the period and the step counter advances by one (modulo 8). One step therefore lasts (period + 1) × 2 CPU cycles. Writing <code>$4003</code>/<code>$4007</code> resets the step to 0 but leaves the timer alone.</p>\n<p><code>NES_APU_PULSE_PARITY</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1 (pulse timers tick on odd CPU cycles); rival 0 (even). The default is tuned (chosen because it works, not derived). Whether this is the same divider phase as the knob <code>jitter_parity</code> is <b>not established on hardware</b>: the core's default treats them independently (pulse on odd cycles; <code>$4017</code> jitter cost on odd cycles with <code>jitter_parity</code> 0); the rival is that they are one phase.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Which parity the chip's divider came up in cannot be derived; no register-reading cartridge can see it. <code>apu_mixer/square</code> and <code>apu_mixer/dmc</code> write the inverse of a pulse wave into the DMC level on cycle-counted schedules written against hardware, so a pulse edge one cycle off leaves a spike at every edge. Measured residual (lower is better): parity 0 gives <code>square</code> −31.5 dB, <code>dmc</code> −33.3 dB; parity 1 gives −35.3 dB and −37.7 dB; both cartridges move the same direction. No test cartridge's verdict moves either way.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">6.3 The sweep unit</h5>\n<p>State per channel: enable, divider period (<code>$4001</code> bits 6-4), negate, shift, reload flag, divider. <b>Pulse 1 negates with ones' complement and pulse 2 with two's complement</b> (measured on hardware: a wiring difference between the two channels; a downward sweep on pulse 1 lands one step lower for its whole run).</p>\n<p><b>The target period</b>, computed whenever it is needed (it is consulted every cycle for muting, not only on sweep clocks):</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>change = period shifted right by the shift count;</li><li>negate clear: target = period + change;</li><li>negate set, pulse 1: target = period − change − 1;</li><li>negate set, pulse 2: target = period − change.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Muting.</b> The channel is silenced (outputs 0) when <b>the period is below 8</b>, or when <b>negate is clear and the target exceeds <code>$7FF</code></b>. This holds whether or not the sweep is enabled (confirmed by the <code>apu_test</code> sweep rows; failure mode of computing it only on a sweep clock: a high-period channel with the sweep disabled keeps sounding).</p>\n<p><b>On each half-frame clock</b> (after the four length counters):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the divider is 0, the sweep is enabled, the shift is non-zero, and the channel is not muted: the period becomes the target.</li><li>Then, if the divider is 0 <b>or</b> the reload flag is set (either or both): the divider is reloaded with the divider period and the reload flag is cleared. Otherwise the divider is decremented.</li></ol>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">6.4 The pulse output</h5>\n<p>Each cycle, a pulse outputs its volume (section 4) when <b>all</b> of: length counter &gt; 0, not muted by the sweep, and the duty pattern's current step is 1. Otherwise 0. Range 0-15.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s7\">7. The triangle</h4>\n<p><code>APU_TRIANGLE_SEQ</code>, 32 steps, four bits wide:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>steps</th><th>values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0</td></tr><tr><td>16-23</td><td>0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7</td></tr><tr><td>24-31</td><td>8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The sequencer is a counter and taps; the values are what the output sees.</p>\n<p><b>Timer.</b> 11-bit period, ticked on <b>every</b> CPU cycle: decrement; when below 0, reload with the period and, if the length counter &gt; 0 <b>and</b> the linear counter &gt; 0, advance the step (modulo 32). One step lasts period + 1 CPU cycles. The step is not reset by any register write and is 0 at power-on.</p>\n<p><b>Linear counter.</b> A <b>quarter-frame</b> unit. On each quarter-frame clock: if the reload flag is set, the counter becomes the reload value (<code>$4008</code> bits 6-0); otherwise, if non-zero, it decrements. Then, if the control bit (<code>$4008</code> bit 7) is clear, the reload flag is cleared. <code>$400B</code> writes set the reload flag (confirmed by <code>apu_test/1-len_ctr</code> and <code>apu_reset/len_ctrs_enabled</code>).</p>\n<p><b>Output.</b> The triangle outputs <code>APU_TRIANGLE_SEQ</code> at its current step, always: when its counters stop it, it holds its step as a level; it is never gated to 0.</p>\n<p><b>Ultrasonic periods.</b> <code>NES_APU_TRI_ULTRASONIC</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the sequencer keeps stepping at period 0 and 1 (a 56 kHz / 28 kHz waveform);</li><li>when both <code>NES_APU_TRI_ULTRASONIC</code> and <code>NES_APU_TND_EXACT</code> are 1, while the period is below 2 and both counters are non-zero, the mixer is handed the waveform's <b>mean, 7.5</b>, instead of the current step (section 12.2, \"index 16\");</li><li>a halted sequencer at those periods still outputs its held step.</li></ol>\n<p>Rival 0: the sequencer does not advance at periods below 2 (it freezes on whatever step it reached) and the mixer always sees the step. The freeze rule is an emulator option, not hardware.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Putting the linear counter on the half-frame clock halves every note's attack. The ultrasonic default follows the sequencer's own clocking rule (the circuit has one clocking rule at every period); what an analog low-pass makes of a ramp it cannot follow is its mean. The reason for the mean substitution: feeding 28-56 kHz steps through the box average and the 14 kHz pole aliases; without it one title measured +0.9 dB in the top octave, and with it the output matches another emulator's.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s8\">8. The noise channel</h4>\n<p><b>Shift register.</b> Fifteen bits, <b>seeded with 1 at power-on</b> (0 is its dead state). When the timer expires: feedback = bit 0 XOR (bit 6 in short mode, bit 1 in long mode); the register shifts right by one and the feedback enters at bit 14. <b>The channel is audible when bit 0 is 0.</b></p>\n<p><b>Timer.</b> The period is the region's table entry minus one, stored at the <code>$400E</code> write. The timer decrements; when below 0 it is reloaded with the stored period and the register shifts. So the register shifts once every <em>table value</em> CPU cycles.</p>\n<p><code>NES_APU_NOISE_FULL_RATE</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1: the noise timer ticks on <b>every</b> CPU cycle. Rival 0: it ticks only on even cycles, doubling every period (the channel then plays one octave low). The default is taken from another emulator's source and the NESdev wiki \"APU Noise\" table, which is labelled in CPU cycles, and measured by audio comparison.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The shortest period is 4 CPU cycles, which a half-rate timer cannot produce.</p></div>\n<p><b>Period tables</b>, CPU cycles per shift, index = <code>$400E</code> bits 3-0:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>table</th><th>index 0-7</th><th>index 8-15</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_NTSC</code></td><td>4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 96, 128, 160</td><td>202, 254, 380, 508, 762, 1016, 2034, 4068</td><td>hardware measurement; the output matches another emulator's</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_PAL</code></td><td>4, 8, 14, 30, 60, 88, 118, 148</td><td>188, 236, 354, 472, 708, 944, 1890, 3778</td><td>another emulator's source</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>On a VS. System cabinet the noise channel has no short mode</b> (<code>NES_R18_VS_EARLY_NOISE</code> = 1): when the machine is in VS mode, <code>$400E</code> bit 7 is ignored (long mode always) and index 15 divides by <b>2046</b> CPU cycles instead of the table's entry. Home cartridges are untouched. Failure mode of honouring the bit on a cabinet: VS titles that set it play a pitched buzz the cabinet's early 2A03 cannot make (measured on hardware: NESdev \"APU Noise\": \"In the earliest revisions of the 2A03 CPU, the periodic noise flag (M bit) was nonexistent ... the lowest period (rate $F) lasts 2046 M2 cycles instead of 4068\", naming Vs. System boards among that revision's users).</p>\n<p><b>PAL uses <code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_PAL</code></b>, chosen at the <code>$400E</code> write when the machine's region is PAL; NTSC and Dendy use the NTSC table. The PAL row is a different set of chip dividers, not a scaling of the NTSC row (taken from another emulator's source: the NESdev wiki \"APU Noise\" PAL row and another emulator's noise channel agree entry for entry; no test cartridge reads it back). In the source, <code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_PAL</code> can be an alias: with the falsifier build switch <code>NES_APU_PAL_NOISE_R14</code> defined, the alias <code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_PAL</code> names a different row instead, which is wrong from index 6 on, matches neither source and has no recorded origin (4, 8, 14, 30, 60, 88 then 148, 200, 254, 320, 476, 636, 956, 1272, 2548, 5092). The shipped core does not define it; build the table above.</p>\n<p><b>Output.</b> Volume (section 4) when the length counter &gt; 0 and register bit 0 is 0; otherwise 0.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s9\">9. The DMC (delta-modulation channel)</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">9.1 Rate tables</h5>\n<p>CPU cycles per output bit, index = <code>$4010</code> bits 3-0. NTSC and Dendy use the NTSC row; PAL the PAL row. There is no formula.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>table</th><th>index 0-7</th><th>index 8-15</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>APU_DMC_RATE_NTSC</code></td><td>428, 380, 340, 320, 286, 254, 226, 214</td><td>190, 160, 142, 128, 106, 84, 72, 54</td><td>test: <code>apu_test/8-dmc_rates</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_DMC_RATE_PAL</code></td><td>398, 354, 316, 298, 276, 236, 210, 198</td><td>176, 148, 132, 118, 98, 78, 66, 50</td><td>hardware measurement</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">9.2 The timer and the output unit</h5>\n<p>State: timer (power-on value 428, the NTSC index-0 rate, on every region), output level (7 bits, 0 at power-on), shift register, bits-remaining counter (0 at power-on), silence flag (clear at power-on), and the one-byte DMC buffer with its full flag.</p>\n<p>Every CPU cycle the timer is decremented; when it reaches <b>0 or below</b> it is reloaded with the current rate table entry for the current rate index, and the output unit is clocked once:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If bits-remaining is 0 (a new output cycle begins): set it to 8; if the buffer is full, move the buffer into the shift register, mark the buffer empty and clear the silence flag; otherwise set the silence flag. Then <b>ask for a byte</b> (section 9.3). <b>A byte fetched in the cycle just before this clock is not yet in the buffer</b> (switch <code>NES_R18_DMC_FETCH_EDGE</code> = 1, placed by a per-cycle trace of another emulator, chosen because the output then matches another emulator's): if the buffer was filled by a fetch in the previous CPU cycle, set the silence flag and leave the byte in the buffer for the next output cycle (the request step then finds the buffer full and asks for nothing). Failure mode: a second fetch two cycles after the first (AccuracyCoin \"Implicit DMA Abort\", third loop). <b>The implicit abort of a one-byte stream</b> (switch <code>NES_R18_DMC_IMPLICIT_ABORT</code> = 1, with <code>NES_R18_DMC_ABORT</code>): when a fetch completes for a stream of length 1 that does not loop, while bits-remaining is 0 and the timer stands at <code>NES_R18_DMC_IMPLICIT_LO</code> = 2 to <code>NES_R18_DMC_IMPLICIT_HI</code> = 3 (both ends included), the stream restarts (one byte remaining again) and the disable counter of section 9.4 is set to 3 if it is not running. The output unit then takes the byte and asks for the next, and the disable lands on that request's halt cycle: a one-cycle fetch, or none if that cycle is a write (the outline follows the NESdev wiki DMA page, \"DMC DMA bugs\"; the window is placed by a per-cycle trace of another emulator, chosen because the output then matches another emulator's; confirmed by AccuracyCoin \"Implicit DMA Abort\", success code 2). At timer 1 one console revision re-plays the byte with a full fetch (AccuracyCoin's other answer key); that revision is <b>not</b> modelled.</li><li>If not silenced: if shift register bit 0 is 1 and the level is ≤ 125, add 2; if bit 0 is 0 and the level is ≥ 2, subtract 2. The level clamps; it never wraps.</li><li>Shift the register right by one; decrement bits-remaining.</li></ol>\n<p><code>apu_test/7-dmc_basics</code> and <code>dmc_tests</code> confirm the output unit.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why</b> (the fetch edge). This core clocks the output unit one cycle after the console edge it models, so that fetch and this clock are the same edge, where the unit reads the buffer before the fetch lands.</p></div>\n<p><b>The other order: the new output cycle in the same clock (<code>NES_DMC_RELOAD_SAME_CLOCK</code> = 0, off by default).</b> An implementer builds it <b>off</b>: steps 1-3 above are the specification. With it on, the output unit's clock is: change the level (unless silenced), shift, decrement bits-remaining, and <b>if it reached 0 in this clock</b>, start the new cycle at once (bits-remaining 8; reload the shifter from the buffer and clear the silence flag, or set the silence flag; ask for a byte); and power-on sets bits-remaining to 8 and the silence flag (a reading: the published description gives 8 but not the flag, and an empty shift register has nothing to play). So every fetch request comes one DMC timer period (one rate-table entry, in CPU cycles) earlier than in the order above.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why</b> (off). This is the order as written on the NESdev wiki (APU DMC, output unit: the steps in that order, and a new cycle starts when bits-remaining reaches zero). No test cartridge decides the reload order: with it on, every DMA test cartridge still passes, and the picture of <code>dpcmletterbox.nes</code> matches another emulator's on fewer frames (290 → 288 at the shipped alignment, 284 → 286 at alignment 4 of <a href=\"#nesemu-00-machine\">00-MACHINE.md</a> 6.6, never 290).</p></div>\n<p>The channel's mixer input is the output level, always (0-127).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">9.3 Asking for a byte, and the fetch</h5>\n<p>A byte is asked for <b>only</b> at step 1 above and when a <code>$4015</code> enable's start delay matures (section 9.4). The request is made when the buffer is empty <b>and</b> bytes remaining &gt; 0 <b>and</b> no start delay is in flight (an in-flight delay is the request that will be made; the buffer emptying meanwhile does not make a second, earlier one). Making the request lowers the CPU's ready line; the fetch is performed by the direct memory access unit at the next CPU <b>read</b> cycle (reads only: a write cannot be interrupted), or inside a running sprite DMA by arbitration. Both procedures are in <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a> (confirmed by <code>dmc_dma_during_read4</code>, <code>sprdma_and_dmc_dma</code>, <code>read_joy3/count_errors</code>).</p>\n<p>When the fetch happens, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the byte at the current address is read over the CPU bus (a real bus read, visible to the cartridge), and the buffer becomes full;</li><li>the address advances by one: <b><code>$FFFF</code> is followed by <code>$8000</code></b>, not <code>$0000</code> (the counter is 15 bits with A15 tied high);</li><li>bytes remaining is decremented;</li><li>if it reached 0: if loop is set the stream restarts (address from <code>$4012</code>, length from <code>$4013</code>); otherwise, if the interrupt enable is set, the <b>DMC interrupt flag is set</b> and its interrupt line asserted.</li></ol>\n<p>The flag is raised when the <b>last byte is fetched</b>, not when it finishes playing.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">9.4 The <code>$4015</code> start delay</h5>\n<p><code>NES_DMC_START_DELAY</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1. When a <code>$4015</code> write with bit 4 set restarts an idle stream (bytes remaining was 0), the first fetch request is <b>not</b> made on the write's own cycle:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>A start-delay counter is set to <b>4 if the write's cycle is a \"get\" cycle of the DMA unit, and 3 if it is a \"put\" cycle</b> (switch <code>NES_R18_DMC_LOAD_APU2</code> = 1; at 0 the get value is 2).</li><li>The counter is decremented on each subsequent tick (step 3 of section 2).</li><li>The request is made on the tick where it reaches 0, and then serviced at the next read, like any other request.</li></ol>\n<p>The request matures on the get that opens the <b>second APU cycle after the one holding the write</b>, \"load DMA after 2 APU cycles\" (measured on hardware; confirmed by AccuracyCoin \"DMA + $2002 Read\", whose <code>LDA $2002</code> meets the load fetch on its read cycle only with 4; with 2 the fetch lands on the operand fetch, two cycles early, and AccuracyCoin \"Delta Modulation Channel\" stays a pass either way). With the default <code>dma_align</code> = 2, get cycles are the <b>odd</b> cycles, so a write on an odd cycle requests 4 ticks later and a write on an even cycle 3 ticks later; with <code>dma_align</code> 1 or 0 the get cycles are the even ones. Rival 0: the request is made immediately during the write.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (defaults).</b> A <code>$4015</code> ← <code>$10</code> write during cycle 2001 (odd, a get) with bytes remaining 0 sets the counter to 4; ticks 2002, 2003, 2004 and 2005 count it 3, 2, 1, 0; the request is made in cycle 2005's tick. The same write during cycle 2000 (even, a put) sets 3; the request is made in cycle 2003's tick.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The parity dependence follows from the hardware: the DMC is clocked on one half of the two-cycle phase, so a write is acted on at that half's next edge. The fixed part (why 2 and 3 rather than 1 and 2, the counts with <code>NES_R18_DMC_LOAD_APU2</code> = 0) is taken from another emulator's source and is not established on hardware. Measured: without the delay, the first fetch after each enable in <code>sprdma_and_dmc_dma</code> lands 1 or 2 cycles early, and with it the output matches another emulator's.</p></div>\n<p><b>A <code>$4015</code> write with bit 4 clear does not act at once</b> (switch <code>NES_R18_DMC_ABORT</code> = 1; that test, AccuracyCoin \"Explicit DMA Abort\", confirms it). The rules:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>A disable counter is set (unless one is already running) to <b><code>NES_R18_DMC_DIS_GET</code> = 3 if the write's cycle is a get, <code>NES_R18_DMC_DIS_PUT</code> = 2 if it is a put</b>.</li><li>It is decremented at the <b>end</b> of each later tick, after the DMC timer and the start delay (step 3 of section 2).</li><li>When it reaches 0: bytes remaining = 0, and a fetch already requested is dealt with by where the CPU stands (<a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a> §9.5):\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>where the CPU stands when the counter reaches 0</th><th>result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>not yet halted, and the cycle is a write</td><td>the request is cancelled</td></tr><tr><td>the cycle is a read that the request is halting</td><td>the halt cycle happens and nothing else (a one-cycle fetch)</td></tr><tr><td>between the halt and the get</td><td>aborted after the alignment cycle if one is still to come, otherwise the get goes ahead</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li><li>A fetch whose get happens after bytes reached 0 puts its address on the bus and the channel takes nothing (no buffer fill, no count).</li><li>The write itself still cancels nothing; an enable written while the counter runs finds bytes remaining non-zero and does not restart.</li><li>With the switch at 0 the write cancels the start delay, the pending request and the owed halt and dummy at once.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The DMC is an APU-side circuit that sees the write at its next clock edge; the placement in this core's cycle accounting is taken from a per-cycle trace of another emulator on AccuracyCoin \"Explicit DMA Abort\", chosen because the output then matches another emulator's. Failure mode of acting at once: AccuracyCoin \"Explicit DMA Abort\" reads <code>… 04 04 00 00 00 …</code> where hardware reads <code>… 03 04 01 01 00 …</code>; the delays the other way round (2 on a get, 3 on a put) fail it too.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s10\">10. The frame counter</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">10.1 The step tables</h5>\n<p>The frame counter is a step counter (power-on value: see section 11) advanced by one on every tick and compared with the current mode's table. Flag bits:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>flag</th><th>value</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>APU_FC_Q</code></td><td>1</td><td>quarter-frame clock: the three envelopes and the triangle's linear counter</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_FC_H</code></td><td>2</td><td>half-frame clock: the four length counters, then the two sweep units</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_FC_I</code></td><td>4</td><td>set the frame interrupt flag (unless inhibited)</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_FC_R</code></td><td>8</td><td>restart: the step counter becomes 0 on this same tick</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In each table row below, the first number is the step-counter value (CPU cycles since the restart) and the following numbers are the flag values present on that step (written separately; a step's effect is their sum). A step with flags 0 does nothing. Within one step the effects run in the order Q, H, I, R.</p>\n<p><code>APU_FC0_NTSC</code>: four-step mode, NTSC and Dendy:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>cycle (step-counter value)</th><th>flags</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7457</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>14913</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>22371</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>29828</td><td>4</td><td>interrupt flag</td></tr><tr><td>29829</td><td>1, 2, 4</td><td>quarter, half, interrupt flag</td></tr><tr><td>29830</td><td>4, 8</td><td>interrupt flag, restart</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>APU_FC1_NTSC</code>: five-step mode, NTSC and Dendy:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>cycle (step-counter value)</th><th>flags</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>7457</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>14913</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>22371</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>29829</td><td>0</td><td>nothing: the step that does nothing</td></tr><tr><td>37281</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>37282</td><td>8</td><td>restart</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>APU_FC0_PAL</code>: four-step mode, PAL:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>cycle (step-counter value)</th><th>flags</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8313</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>16627</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>24939</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>33252</td><td>4</td><td>interrupt flag</td></tr><tr><td>33253</td><td>1, 2, 4</td><td>quarter, half, interrupt flag</td></tr><tr><td>33254</td><td>4, 8</td><td>interrupt flag, restart</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>APU_FC1_PAL</code>: five-step mode, PAL:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>cycle (step-counter value)</th><th>flags</th><th>effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8313</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>16627</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>24939</td><td>1</td><td>quarter</td></tr><tr><td>33253</td><td>0</td><td>nothing</td></tr><tr><td>41565</td><td>1, 2</td><td>quarter, half</td></tr><tr><td>41566</td><td>8</td><td>restart</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Confirmed on NTSC by <code>blargg_apu_2005.07.30</code> 05.len_timing_mode0, 06.len_timing_mode1, 07.irq_flag_timing, 08.irq_timing and <code>apu_test/5-len_timing</code>; on PAL by the ten <code>pal_apu_tests</code> rows.</p>\n<p><b>The restart step is also step 0 of the next period</b>: the counter is set to 0 on the restart tick and is 1 on the next, so the four-step period is 29830 CPU cycles (PAL 33254) and the five-step 37282 (PAL 41566), and the next quarter clock falls 7457 ticks after the restart tick. PAL has the same shape with different dividers, which is why PAL is a timing mode and not a second unit (confirmed by the same tests).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">10.2 The frame interrupt flag</h5>\n<p>On a step carrying flag 4:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>inhibit bit (<code>$4017</code> bit 6, as currently latched)</th><th>first and second of the three steps</th><th>third step (the one that also carries flag 8, the restart)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>clear</td><td>flag set, frame interrupt line asserted</td><td>flag set, line asserted</td></tr><tr><td>set</td><td>flag set, line <b>not</b> asserted</td><td>flag cleared</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So with interrupts inhibited <code>$4015</code> bit 6 reads 1 for exactly two cycles and no interrupt is ever raised. Switch <code>NES_R18_FRAME_IRQ_PHASE</code> = 1 (measured on consoles by AccuracyCoin's author; confirmed by AccuracyCoin \"Frame Counter IRQ\" tests I-M). Failure mode of doing nothing while inhibited: those two cycles read 0 (tests J and K). With the switch at 0, nothing happens while inhibited. Because three consecutive steps carry flag 4, <b>the flag is set on three consecutive CPU cycles</b> (29828, 29829, 29830 on NTSC); <code>07.irq_flag_timing</code> checks the first and the last.</p>\n<p>The flag (and its line) is cleared by: a <code>$4015</code> read (section 3.6); a <code>$4017</code> write with bit 6 set (immediately, during the write); power-on and reset. Nothing else clears it; in particular not a <code>$4015</code> write and not a <code>$4017</code> write with bit 6 clear.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">10.3 The <code>$4017</code> write delay and its jitter</h5>\n<p>A <code>$4017</code> write during cycle N:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>remembers the value (for reset);</li><li>latches bit 6 as the inhibit immediately, and clears the frame flag if it is 1;</li><li>sets the delay counter to d = <code>write_delay</code> (knob 0, default 3), <b>plus 1 if the parity of N differs from <code>jitter_parity</code></b> (knob 1, default 0). With the defaults, a write on an even cycle has d = 3 and on an odd cycle d = 4;</li><li>remembers the value as the pending one (its bit 7 is the new mode).</li></ol>\n<p>A second <code>$4017</code> write while one is in flight replaces the pending value and restarts the delay.</p>\n<p>On each tick while the delay counter is non-zero:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the delay counter is decremented first;</li><li>if it is still non-zero, <b>the old sequence keeps running</b>: the step counter advances and its steps (including interrupt steps) fire as usual;</li><li>if it reached 0, the <b>restart</b> happens and nothing else happens in the frame counter on that tick: the mode becomes the pending bit 7, the step counter becomes 0, and <b>if the new mode is five-step, one quarter-frame and one half-frame clock are performed immediately</b>. Four-step mode does not clock at the restart. <code>apu_test/1-len_ctr</code> items 4 and 5 check both halves.</li></ol>\n<p>So with the defaults the restart is at the tick of cycle N+3 (N even) or N+4 (N odd), and the first quarter clock at N+3+7457 or N+4+7457.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (NTSC, defaults).</b> <code>$4017</code> ← <code>$00</code> during cycle 1000 (even): d = 3; restart on cycle 1003's tick (step counter 0); quarter on cycle 8460; quarter and half on 15916; quarter on 23374; frame flag set on 30831, 30832, 30833; restart again on 30833.</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>write  restart       quarter        flag set        restart\n1000   1003 (=0)     1003+7457      1003+29828..30   1003+29830</code></pre>\n<p>The write delay 3 is confirmed from both sides by the 2005 suite. The jitter parity 0 is <b>not established on hardware</b>: the core's default is 0 (the extra cycle is paid on odd cycles), the rival 1 (paid on even cycles). Measured: 49 audio test cartridges, both values pass.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> A sweep of the write delay over 1..4 against the 2005 suite: 2 makes 04-08 report \"too soon\", 4 makes 04, 05, 06, 08 report \"too late\" and fails 01; opposite directions, so 3 is pinned. Every cartridge that cares synchronises to the jitter first, so both jitter settings pass all 49 audio rows.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s11\">11. Power-on and reset</h4>\n<p><b>At power-on</b> the whole unit is zeroed <b>except the knobs</b> (which keep whatever the host set after initialisation; the knobs' defaults are applied at initialisation, not at power-on), and then:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>item</th><th>power-on value</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mixer pulse table</td><td>built (section 12.1)</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>output divider</td><td><b>40</b> (overwrites any divider set before power-on)</td><td>tuned: about 44.7 kHz at the NTSC rate</td></tr><tr><td>pulse 1 negate mode</td><td>ones' complement (pulse 2 two's)</td><td>hardware measurement</td></tr><tr><td>noise shift register</td><td>1</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>noise period (stored reload value)</td><td><b>3</b>: table entry 0 (4 CPU cycles) minus one, because <code>$400E</code> powers on as 0 and 0 is an index, not a divider. <code>NES_R18_NOISE_POWERON</code> = 1. Failure mode of leaving it 0: until the first <code>$400E</code> write the shift register steps twice as often as index 0 allows (measured: 2,000 steps in 4,000 cycles instead of 1,000)</td><td>hardware rule (NESdev \"CPU power up state\": <code>$400C</code>-<code>$400F</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>remembered <code>$4017</code> value</td><td><code>$00</code></td><td>hardware measurement</td></tr><tr><td>DMC stream start address</td><td><code>$C000</code></td><td>hardware rule (<code>$4012</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>DMC stream length</td><td>1</td><td>hardware rule (<code>$4013</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>DMC timer</td><td>428</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>pending halt flags</td><td>none</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td>everything else (timers, steps, periods, envelopes, sweeps, length counters, linear counter, output level, filter state, sample queue, step counter)</td><td>0</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>At both power-on and reset</b> (reset does not zero anything else: <code>$4000</code>-<code>$4013</code> survive a reset, <code>apu_reset/len_ctrs_enabled</code>), in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><code>$4015</code> is cleared: all four enables 0, all four length counters 0, DMC bytes remaining 0, pending fetch request, start delay and owed DMA halt/dummy cycles cancelled (confirmed by <code>apu_reset/4015_cleared</code>).</li><li>Both interrupt flags cleared and both interrupt lines released.</li><li><b>At reset only</b> (<code>NES_R18_APU_RESET_DMC_TRI</code> = 1): the DMC's output level keeps only bit 0 (<code>level &amp;= 1</code>), and the triangle's step counter returns to 0 (output 15). Nothing else in the unit is touched. Failure mode of leaving them: a warm reset during a DMC-heavy song keeps the output at its last level, a step on the mix, and the triangle resumes mid-waveform (measured on hardware, one console: NESdev \"CPU power up state\", an NTSC front-loader, RP2A03G, NES-CPU-07: after reset \"[$4011] &amp;= 1\" and triangle phase \"0 (output = 15)\").</li><li><b>The implicit <code>$4017</code> write.</b> Value v = <code>$00</code> at power-on, the <b>last value written</b> to <code>$4017</code> at reset. The mode (v bit 7) and the inhibit (v bit 6) are latched <b>directly</b>, with no write delay, and any in-flight <code>$4017</code> write is dropped. The step counter is preset to <code>power_delay</code> − 11 when <code>power_delay</code> (knob 2, default 11) is at least 11, otherwise to 0. If the mode is five-step, one quarter and one half-frame clock are performed now (visible at reset, where length counters may be loaded; invisible at power-on).</li></ol>\n<p>This happens <b>before</b> the CPU's seven reset cycles, which tick normally and advance the step counter. With the default the step counter is 0 before the reset sequence and 7 when the first instruction's opcode fetch begins; at power-on the first quarter clock is therefore on cycle 7457 and the first frame flag set on cycle 29828. <code>apu_reset/4017_timing</code> confirms it: it accepts 9 to 12 and prints its measurement; 11 prints in range; 13 and above fail it and <code>09.reset_timing</code> (\"fourth step occurs too soon\"). Below 11 the value is not established on hardware: the default is 11, the rival 9-10 (not reachable in this core).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The knob is bounded only from above by measurement: values below 11 all produce the same machine (the floor), because the reset sequence's seven cycles are real cycles and the write cannot be placed before them. 9, the usual cold-unit figure, is not expressible here.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s12\">12. The mixer</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">12.1 The pulse group</h5>\n<p>The two pulse outputs (0-15 each) are summed and looked up in a 31-entry table built at power-on:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>pulse(i) = 95.88 / (8128 / i + 100) for i &gt; 0, pulse(0) = 0; entry = pulse(i) × 32767, truncated toward zero.</p></div>\n<p>All arithmetic is single-precision floating point (32-bit IEEE), evaluated as written: 8128 divided by i, plus 100, 95.88 divided by that, multiplied by 32767, truncated. The resulting table, in mixer units (a worked example an implementer can check against, computed that way):</p>\n<pre class=\"gbemu-pre\"><code>i  0-7 :    0   381   754  1118  1473  1820  2159  2491\ni  8-15: 2815  3131  3441  3744  4041  4332  4616  4894\ni 16-23: 5167  5434  5696  5952  6203  6450  6692  6929\ni 24-30: 7161  7390  7614  7833  8049  8261  8469</code></pre>\n<p>Entry 15 is 4894. The coefficients are measured on hardware: they are fitted to measured output levels (NESdev wiki \"APU Mixer\").</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The shape follows from the hardware: the channel groups drive a resistor ladder into one node, so the sum is a parallel combination, not an addition.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">12.2 The triangle / noise / DMC group</h5>\n<p><code>NES_APU_TND_EXACT</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1, the exact form, with t = triangle (0-15, or the special value 16 meaning 7.5, section 7), n = noise (0-15), d = DMC level (0-127):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>sum = (t / 8227 + n / 12241) + d / 22638; tnd = 159.79 / (1 / sum + 100) when sum &gt; 0, else 0; value = tnd × 32767, truncated toward zero.</p></div>\n<p>Single precision, evaluated in that order (t/8227 plus n/12241 first, then plus d/22638; then 1 divided by sum, plus 100; then 159.79 divided by that; then × 32767). When t is 16 the value 7.5 is used in place of t. The value may be cached against the last (t, n, d) since it is a pure function of them. Worked examples, in mixer units: (15, 0, 0) → 8074; (0, 15, 0) → 5715; (0, 0, 127) → 18816; (15, 15, 127) → 24297; (16, 0, 0) → 4374; (1, 0, 0) → 628; (0, 0, 1) → 230.</p>\n<p>Rival 0, the approximation: a 203-entry table, entry(i) = 163.67 / (24329 / i + 100) × 32767 (truncated, entry 0 = 0), indexed by 3t + 2n + d. Both forms are taken from another emulator's source and the NESdev wiki \"APU Mixer\" page, which gives both and calls the table an approximation; the coefficients are measured on hardware.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The approximation weighs the triangle as exactly 3 DMC-level steps and the noise as 2, where the circuit gives 2.75 and 1.85. <code>apu_mixer/triangle</code> and <code>apu_mixer/square</code> cancel a tone against its inverse through the DMC level; with the approximation the residual is 12 dB louder than another emulator's, the triangle 9 % too strong and the DMC level's small-signal gain 5 % too weak, and with the exact form the output matches another emulator's.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">12.3 The node, and where expansion audio joins</h5>\n<p>Each CPU cycle the unsigned mixer value is:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>mix = pulse_table[pulse1 + pulse2] + tnd(t, n, d) + every cartridge or disk source whose leg below is \"unsigned mix\"</p></div>\n<p>as an unsigned integer sum. Every source outside the 2A03 joins by exactly one of the legs in this table, chosen by the mapper number; a source whose mute bit (below) is set adds nothing to any leg, and the chip itself still runs:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>source</th><th>joins when</th><th>leg</th><th>sign</th><th>mute bit</th><th>switch (default)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disk System wavetable</td><td>every mapper (its level is 0 while <code>$4023</code> bit 1 is 0, <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>)</td><td>the Disk System leg (section 13.1 step 4)</td><td>added</td><td>5</td><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_LOWPASS</code> = 1; at 0 it is added to the unsigned mix</td></tr><tr><td>MMC5 audio</td><td>mapper 5</td><td>the signed leg (<code>xs_accum</code>)</td><td><b>subtracted</b></td><td>6</td><td><code>NES_R18_MMC5_INVERT</code> = 1; at 0 it is added to the unsigned mix</td></tr><tr><td>Study Box tape</td><td>mapper 186</td><td>the signed leg</td><td>added (a signed level)</td><td>11</td><td><code>NES_R18_STUDYBOX</code> = 1</td></tr><tr><td>DripGame's two PCM channels</td><td>mapper 284</td><td>the signed leg</td><td>added (a signed level)</td><td>12</td><td><code>NES_R20_NCH</code> = 1</td></tr><tr><td>Konami VRC6</td><td>mappers 24 and 26</td><td>the signed leg</td><td><b>subtracted</b></td><td>7</td><td><code>NES_R18_VRC6_INVERT</code> = 1, a tuned default (chosen because it works, not derived); at 0 it is added to the unsigned mix</td></tr><tr><td>Sunsoft 5B</td><td>mapper 69</td><td>the 5B leg (section 13.1 step 3)</td><td>added</td><td>10</td><td><code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code> = 1; at 0 it is added to the unsigned mix</td></tr><tr><td>Konami VRC7 FM</td><td>mapper 85</td><td>the unsigned mix</td><td>added</td><td>8</td><td>(none)</td></tr><tr><td>Namco 163</td><td>mapper 19</td><td>the unsigned mix</td><td>added</td><td>9</td><td>(none)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The cartridge and disk channels join here, at the output node, and nowhere earlier</b>: they are added, not folded into either non-linear group. Their levels are owned by <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>, which states each (the Study Box's in <a href=\"#nesemu-44-mappers-tail\">44-MAPPERS-TAIL.md</a>, Mapper 186, and DripGame's with mapper 284 in the same chapter). The Disk System, VRC6, Sunsoft 5B and Namco 163 levels come from another emulator's mixer weights converted to these units (chosen because the output then matches another emulator's; one unit of those weights = 32767 / 5000 = 6.5534 mixer units): full scales 8257, 30012, 52020 and 1048 … 30538 (silent level 16776). The VRC7 scales from <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> = 4893 (defined with the mapper chips), the stated assumption that a cartridge channel at full volume reaches the node at the same amplitude as one 2A03 pulse at volume 15, the pulse table's entry 15 (which is 4894; the constant is 4893). That level is not established on hardware: a consistency argument, not a measurement; default 4893, rival: any measured level. The unsigned sum is accumulated in 32 bits per output period; the largest per-cycle mix (VRC7, about 177,000) leaves room for any divider below about 24,000, and no title measured clamps after power-on.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The expansion sources are on a different board with their own leg into the mixer, so they are added, not folded into either non-linear group. A signed source (the Study Box tape, DripGame's channels) is centred on zero; in the unsigned sum it would need an offset, and the offset would step the output at power-on.</p></div>\n<p><b>The mute mask</b> (<code>nes_apu_set_mute</code>, instrument only, 0 at power-on, never set by the host) holds <b>13 bits</b> (the call keeps value AND <code>$1FFF</code>). It zeroes inputs at the node only: bit 0 pulse 1, bit 1 pulse 2, bit 2 triangle, bit 3 noise, bit 4 DMC; and the outside sources of the table above, bit 5 Disk System, 6 MMC5, 7 VRC6, 8 VRC7, 9 Namco 163, 10 Sunsoft 5B, 11 Study Box tape, 12 DripGame. Registers, counters and DMA are unaffected.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s13\">13. The output stage: averaging and the console's filters</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">13.1 Averaging (decimation)</h5>\n<p>There is no point-sampling and no resampler. Every CPU cycle the unsigned mix of section 12.3 is added to a 32-bit accumulator and a cycle count is incremented; the signed leg, the 5B leg and the Disk System leg are each added to their own accumulator the same cycle. When the count reaches the <b>divider</b> (<code>nes_audio_set_divider</code>; 40 after power-on), one output sample is produced, in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>The unsigned mix.</b> x = accumulator ÷ count (unsigned integer division, then converted to float).</li><li><b>The signed leg</b> (<code>xs_accum</code>, a signed 32-bit accumulator: the Study Box tape and DripGame added, the MMC5 and VRC6 levels subtracted, section 12.3). If it is non-zero, x = x + (accumulator as float ÷ count as float) (single precision). The accumulator is reset to 0.</li><li><b>The 5B leg</b> (mapper 69 only, with <code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code> = 1; the 5B's level summed apart, an unsigned 32-bit accumulator). All single precision:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>s = accumulator ÷ count; reset the accumulator to 0.</li><li>h = k5hp × (previous h + s − previous s), a one-pole high-pass at <code>S5B_AMP_HP_HZ</code> = 90 Hz; remember h and s.</li><li>peak = |h|.</li><li>the envelope (state, 0 at power-on): if peak &gt; envelope, envelope = peak (immediate attack); otherwise envelope = envelope + k5rel × (peak − envelope).</li><li>g = 1; if envelope &gt; <code>S5B_AMP_THRESHOLD</code> = 20600, g = (envelope ÷ threshold)^(1 ÷ <code>S5B_AMP_RATIO</code> − 1), <code>S5B_AMP_RATIO</code> = 2.7, computed as e^((1/2.7 − 1) × ln(envelope ÷ threshold)).</li><li>x = x + s + h × (g − 1). Under the threshold this is x + s, exactly the linear leg.</li></ol></li><li><b>The Disk System leg</b> (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_LOWPASS</code> = 1; an unsigned 32-bit accumulator). Skipped while both its accumulator and fds_lp are exactly 0. Otherwise: x_fds = accumulator ÷ count (single precision); reset the accumulator; fds_lp = fds_lp + kfds × (x_fds − fds_lp), <code>FDS_LOWPASS_HZ</code> = 2000; x = x + fds_lp. fds_lp is kept as state (0 at power-on).</li><li><b>The filters</b> of section 13.2, then the sample is stored (section 13.3); the unsigned accumulator and the count are reset to 0.</li></ol>\n<p>The coefficients of steps 3 and 4 are computed with the other coefficients (section 13.2): kfds = 1 − e^(−2π·2000/rate), k5hp = e^(−2π·90/rate), k5rel = 1 − e^(−1/(<code>S5B_AMP_RELEASE_S</code> × rate)) with <code>S5B_AMP_RELEASE_S</code> = 0.030 seconds; in the rival form (<code>NES_APU_FILTER_EXACT</code> = 0) kfds = 6.2832·2000/rate, k5hp = 1 − 6.2832·90/rate and k5rel = 1/(0.030 × rate). The logarithm of step 3.5 is computed without a maths library for y ≥ 1: halve y while y ≥ 2, counting the halvings k; then u = (y − 1)/(y + 1) and ln y = k × 0.69314718 + 2u(1 + u²(1/3 + u²(1/5 + u²(1/7 + u²/9)))), single precision (relative error below 1e-6).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (5B leg).</b> Three channels at fixed level 14 swing the 5B leg 3 × 180 × 68 = 36720 mixer units peak to peak, a centred peak of 18360: below 20600, so g = 1 and the leg is linear. If the envelope stands at 26010, g = (26010 ÷ 20600)^(1/2.7 − 1) = e^(−0.6296 × 0.2332) ≈ 0.863, and h is added scaled by g − 1 ≈ −0.137, so the leg's AC part leaves at about 0.863 of its size.</p>\n<p>The output rate is CPU rate ÷ divider: 1789773 ÷ 40 ≈ 44744 Hz on NTSC, 1662607 ÷ 40 ≈ 41565 Hz on PAL, 1773448 ÷ 40 ≈ 44336 Hz on Dendy. Dendy's processor runs at 26,601,712 ÷ 15 Hz. The host resamples to the device rate, and it must take the rate of the region the core reports after the mount (<code>nes_region_get</code>), not the NTSC figure: a host whose emulation is paced by its audio ring runs a PAL machine 1789773 ÷ 1662607 = 7.65% fast (about 53.8 frames a second, 1.3 semitones sharp) if it believes the NTSC rate, and a Dendy machine 0.92% fast (this follows from the clock dividers, and was measured in a browser on a host that made the mistake).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Averaging is a box filter, the cheapest honest anti-alias, chosen as a design decision: it is why a high-frequency square does not alias into a whistle. The Disk System leg is skipped while silent so that a cartridge's output is bit-for-bit what it is without the leg. The 5B and Disk System legs are explained with their chips in <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">13.2 The three filters</h5>\n<p>Two one-pole high-passes (90 Hz, 440 Hz) and one one-pole low-pass (14 kHz), in that order, all in single precision. The corners come from the NTSC console's output stage as documented. The 90 Hz and 14 kHz stages follow parts on the schematic. The 440 Hz stage is a fit to one recording, tied to no part, and its source page withdrew it in 2026 without a new measurement. Switch <code>NES_R25_APU_HP440</code>, default 1, keeps it; with 0, y2 = y1. With it, the output agrees with the pinned reference emulator's in level within 0.03 dB on the comparison's Contra item; without it, it is 3.7 dB louder there.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The console drives the mixer node through a coupling capacitor, so silence is a DC level and what leaves the machine is the change; silence must read as 0.</p></div>\n<p><b>The rate</b> used for the coefficients is 1789773 ÷ divider, or, when the machine is PAL and <code>NES_APU_FILTER_EXACT</code> is 1, 1662607 ÷ divider (single-precision division). Dendy uses the NTSC figure.</p>\n<p><code>NES_APU_FILTER_EXACT</code> = 1: registered choice. Default 1, the exact one-pole discretisation:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>k90 = e^(−2π·90/rate), k440 = e^(−2π·440/rate), k14k = 1 − e^(−2π·14000/rate), with 2π written as 6.2831853 and each argument computed as (−6.2831853 × fc) ÷ rate;</li><li>the coefficients are recomputed only when the divider or the PAL-ness changes (they are a pure function of both, so an implementation may recompute them every sample with the same result).</li></ul>\n<p><b>The exponential</b> is computed without a maths library, for x ≤ 0: halve x (counting the halvings, k) while x is below −0.015625 (−1/64); then r = 1 + x(1 + x(0.5 + x(1/6 + x(1/24 + x/120)))) evaluated innermost first, each constant a single-precision value (1/6 and 1/24 and 1/120 as single-precision quotients); then square r, k times. All in single precision. Relative error below 1e-6 on [−2, 0].</p>\n<p>Worked example, divider 40: NTSC rate 44744.324, k90 = 0.98744136, k440 = 0.94008338, k14k = 0.85997599; PAL rate 41565.176, k90 = 0.98648733, k440 = 0.93565142, k14k = 0.87952334.</p>\n<p>Rival 0, the small-angle form: k90 = 1 − 6.2832·90/rate, k440 = 1 − 6.2832·440/rate, k14k = 6.2832·14000/rate clamped to at most 1, always the NTSC rate, even on PAL. At a 44.7 kHz output that low-pass coefficient is 1.97, clamped to 1: no low-pass at all. Both forms are the one-pole RC step response, sampled.</p>\n<p><b>Per sample</b> (state: hp90 output and input, hp440 output and input, lp, all 0 at power-on):</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>y1 = k90 × ((previous y1 + x) − previous x); remember y1 and x.</li><li>y2 = k440 × ((previous y2 + y1) − previous y1); remember y2 and y1. (With <code>NES_R25_APU_HP440</code> = 0: y2 = y1.)</li><li>lp = lp + k14k × (y2 − lp).</li><li>The sample is lp truncated toward zero to an integer, clamped to −32768 … 32767, stored as <b>signed 16-bit</b>.</li></ol>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">13.3 The sample queue</h5>\n<p>A ring of <b>4096</b> signed 16-bit samples with a write count and a read count (both 32-bit, both 0 at power-on). Each emitted sample is stored at write count modulo 4096 and the write count advances; a 64-bit total of samples emitted is kept. The writer never waits: if the host falls more than 4096 samples behind, the oldest unread samples are overwritten, <code>nes_audio_available</code> still reports write − read, and reading returns whatever the ring now holds at those positions. <b>The queue is a deadline, not a buffer</b>: not pulling loses samples.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>export</th><th>behaviour</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>nes_audio_read</code></td><td>copies up to <code>max</code> samples, oldest first, from read position to write position, advances the read count, returns how many were copied</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_audio_available</code></td><td>write count − read count (unsigned 32-bit)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_audio_emitted</code></td><td>total samples emitted since power-on (64-bit)</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_audio_set_divider</code></td><td>sets the divider; 0 is taken as 1. <b>Power-on resets it to 40</b>, so a value set before <code>nes_power_on</code> is lost; the host passes 40</td></tr><tr><td><code>nes_apu_set_mute</code></td><td>instrument mute mask, low 13 bits: the five channels and the eight outside sources (section 12.3)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The full export list, including the instrument counters (<code>nes_apu_dma_stalls</code>, <code>nes_apu_dma_fetches</code>, <code>nes_apu_joy_glitches</code>, <code>nes_apu_reloads_blocked</code>, <code>nes_apu_frame_cycle</code>, <code>nes_apu_frame_irq</code>, <code>nes_apu_dmc_irq</code>, <code>nes_apu_length</code>) and the knob calls, is in <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s14\">14. The knobs</h4>\n<p>Knobs are integers a sweep can move, set with <code>nes_set_apu_knob(ctx, id, value)</code> after initialisation and before power-on (or at any time), read with <code>nes_apu_knob</code>, counted by <code>nes_apu_knob_count</code> (8), and named <b>by the core</b> through <code>nes_apu_knob_name</code>; no tool keeps its own list. <b>They are an instrument, not configuration</b>: each exists so that a constant can be shown to fail in opposite directions on either side of its value. Defaults are applied at <code>nes_init</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>id (enum)</th><th>name</th><th>default</th><th>meaning, and what pins it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>APU_P_WRITE_DELAY</code> = 0</td><td><code>write_delay</code></td><td>3</td><td>CPU cycles from a <code>$4017</code> write to the restart (section 10.3). confirmed from both sides by the 2005 suite</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_JITTER_PARITY</code> = 1</td><td><code>jitter_parity</code></td><td>0</td><td>the cycle parity on which the <code>$4017</code> delay is <b>not</b> lengthened. not established on hardware: invisible to every test cartridge</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_POWER_DELAY</code> = 2</td><td><code>power_delay</code></td><td>11</td><td>step-counter preset for the implicit <code>$4017</code> write, as <code>power_delay</code> − 11 (section 11). confirmed from above by <code>apu_reset/4017_timing</code>; 11 is this core's floor</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_DMA_CYCLES</code> = 3</td><td><code>dma_cycles</code></td><td>4</td><td>base cost in CPU cycles of a DMC fetch outside a sprite DMA, counting the halt cycle, the dummy, the get and the CPU's repeated read (the CPU's access lands <code>dma_cycles</code> − 1 cycles late, plus alignment). Confirmed by <code>dmc_dma_during_read4</code> (<code>dma_4016_read</code>, <code>dma_2007_read</code>) and <code>sprdma_and_dmc_dma</code>. Procedure in <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a></td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_DMA_ALIGN</code> = 4</td><td><code>dma_align</code></td><td>2</td><td>the DMA unit's get/put phase: 2 = an extra alignment cycle when the halt cycle is <b>even</b>, equivalently \"a get is an odd cycle\"; 1 = when the halt cycle is odd (gets even); 0 = never (no phase; a Gate 0 setting; the get/put test of sections 3.6 and 9.4 then takes gets as even). Also decides the start delay's get test (section 9.4). Confirmed by <code>dma_4016_read</code>, which prints which of five alignments loses a bit: hardware <code>08 08 07 08 08</code>, only 2 puts it third</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_JOY_GLITCH</code> = 5</td><td><code>joy_glitch</code></td><td>1</td><td>1 = a fetch that halts on a read of <code>$4016</code>/<code>$4017</code> clocks that controller port once more, losing exactly one bit. Confirmed by <code>dma_4016_read</code> and <code>read_joy3/count_errors</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_RELOAD_OFFSET</code> = 6</td><td><code>reload_offset</code></td><td>1</td><td>k of the reload-block prediction (section 5.3), in ticks. confirmed from both sides (1 against 2) by <code>11.len_reload_timing</code>, both regions</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P_ENABLED</code> = 7</td><td><code>enabled</code></td><td>1</td><td>Gate 0 control: 0 switches the whole unit off (section 2). On because the hardware has an audio unit</td></tr><tr><td><code>APU_P__COUNT</code> = 8</td><td>(none)</td><td>(none)</td><td>the number of knobs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>There is no knob for the cost of a DMC fetch inside a sprite DMA: the arbitration produces that cost structurally (section 9.3 and <a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>).</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s15\">15. Registered choices, collected</h4>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>name</th><th>value (default)</th><th>rival</th><th>basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NES_APU_PULSE_PARITY</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (pulse timers on even cycles)</td><td>tuned (section 6.2)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_APU_NOISE_FULL_RATE</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (noise timer every second cycle)</td><td>another emulator's source, and measured (section 8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_APU_TRI_ULTRASONIC</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (sequencer frozen below period 2; no mean substitution)</td><td>hardware rule (section 7)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_APU_TND_EXACT</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (the 203-entry approximation)</td><td>another emulator's source; coefficients measured on hardware (section 12.2)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_APU_FILTER_EXACT</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (small-angle coefficients, clamped low-pass, NTSC rate always)</td><td>hardware rule (section 13.2)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_DMC_START_DELAY</code></td><td>1</td><td>0 (first fetch requested during the <code>$4015</code> write)</td><td>hardware rule, and another emulator's source (section 9.4)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_DMC_RELOAD_SAME_CLOCK</code></td><td>0 (off)</td><td>1 (the new output cycle starts in the clock that empties the old one)</td><td>hardware rule (NESdev wiki); off: with it, <code>dpcmletterbox.nes</code> matches another emulator's picture on fewer frames (section 9.2)</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_APU_PAL_NOISE_R14</code></td><td>not defined</td><td>defined: the alias <code>APU_NOISE_PERIOD_PAL</code> names the other PAL row</td><td>falsifier (section 8)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-30-apu-s16\">16. Glossary</h4>\n<p>This glossary covers this chapter and <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a>. Each term is used only in the sense given here.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>term</th><th>meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CPU cycle</td><td>one cycle of the 2A03's processor clock; the unit of every period, delay and timer in both chapters unless a table says otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>cycle N</td><td>the CPU cycle during which the counter <code>cycles</code> reads N; <code>cycles</code> is 0 at power-on and incremented at the start of each cycle (section 1)</td></tr><tr><td>parity</td><td><code>cycles</code> modulo 2 (odd or even cycle)</td></tr><tr><td>audio tick, tick</td><td>the audio unit's work for one CPU cycle (section 2), run after the cartridge's clock and before the cycle's bus access. In <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">35-EXPANSION-AUDIO.md</a> the bare word is not used; \"5B tick\" and \"sequencer tick\" are their own terms</td></tr><tr><td>get cycle, put cycle</td><td>the two halves of the DMA unit's two-cycle phase. With <code>dma_align</code> = 2 (the default) a get is an odd cycle and a put an even one; with 1 or 0, gets are even (section 14)</td></tr><tr><td>put→get transition</td><td>the end of a put cycle (after its bus access, before the interrupt lines are polled); the frame-flag clear of section 3.6 lands here</td></tr><tr><td>quarter-frame clock, half-frame clock</td><td>the frame counter's step flags 1 and 2 (section 10.1): quarter clocks the envelopes and the triangle's linear counter; half clocks the length counters and the sweeps</td></tr><tr><td>step counter</td><td>the frame counter's count of ticks since its last restart (section 10.1)</td></tr><tr><td>frame interrupt flag</td><td><code>$4015</code> bit 6; set by the frame counter (section 10.2)</td></tr><tr><td>DMC interrupt flag</td><td><code>$4015</code> bit 7; set when the last byte of a non-looping stream is fetched (section 9.3). The MMC5's PCM interrupt flag is a different flag, read at <code>$5010</code></td></tr><tr><td>DMC</td><td>the 2A03's delta-modulation channel, <code>$4010</code>-<code>$4013</code> (the identifiers <code>APU_DMC_RATE_NTSC</code>, <code>NES_R18_DMC_…</code>, <code>IRQ_DMC</code> name it)</td></tr><tr><td>stream (DMC)</td><td>the bytes the DMC plays: <code>$4013</code> × 16 + 1 bytes from address <code>$C000</code> + <code>$4012</code> × 64 (section 3.4)</td></tr><tr><td>DMC fetch</td><td>the reading of one stream byte by the direct memory access unit (section 9.3)</td></tr><tr><td>PCM</td><td>output of raw levels written as bytes, with no waveform unit: <code>$4011</code> writes, the MMC5's 8-bit PCM level, DripGame's two PCM channels, a 5B level register used that way</td></tr><tr><td>fetch request</td><td>the DMC's demand for one byte, which lowers the CPU's ready line (section 9.3)</td></tr><tr><td>halt cycle</td><td>the CPU read cycle on which a fetch request stops the CPU (<a href=\"#nesemu-10-cpu\">10-CPU.md</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>start delay</td><td>the counter that postpones the first fetch request after a <code>$4015</code> enable (section 9.4)</td></tr><tr><td>disable counter</td><td>the counter that postpones a <code>$4015</code> disable (section 9.4)</td></tr><tr><td>clear request</td><td>the record a <code>$4015</code> read leaves; the frame flag drops at the next put→get transition (section 3.6)</td></tr><tr><td>muted (pulse)</td><td>silenced by the sweep unit's period test (section 6.3)</td></tr><tr><td>knob</td><td>a runtime integer set with <code>nes_set_apu_knob</code> (section 14)</td></tr><tr><td>switch</td><td>a build-time define (<code>NES_…</code>, <code>S5B_…</code> and so on) with a default; \"at 0\" means the define set to 0</td></tr><tr><td>registered choice</td><td>a switch whose value no test decides; the core's default is stated and must be matched, the other value is the rival</td></tr><tr><td>rival</td><td>the other value of a registered choice or switch</td></tr><tr><td>falsifier</td><td>a switch that exists only so that a check can be seen to fail; every real build uses its default</td></tr><tr><td>mixer unit</td><td>the unit of every level at the node: 32767 is a normalised mixer output of 1.0</td></tr><tr><td>node</td><td>the console's audio mixing point, where the 2A03's two groups and every outside source meet (section 12.3)</td></tr><tr><td>unsigned mix</td><td>the per-cycle unsigned integer sum at the node (section 12.3)</td></tr><tr><td>signed leg</td><td><code>xs_accum</code>, the signed accumulator added at the output divider (section 13.1 step 2)</td></tr><tr><td>5B leg, Disk System leg</td><td>the 5B's and the Disk System's own accumulators, processed at the output divider (section 13.1 steps 3 and 4)</td></tr><tr><td>divider, output divider</td><td>the number of CPU cycles averaged into one output sample (40 after power-on)</td></tr><tr><td>sample, output sample</td><td>one signed 16-bit value placed in the sample queue (section 13); the only meaning of \"sample\" in both chapters</td></tr><tr><td>open bus</td><td>the last value on the data bus; an undriven bit reads its bit of that value</td></tr><tr><td>outside source, expansion chip</td><td>a sound source not in the 2A03: a cartridge chip, the Disk System's wavetable channel, the Study Box tape or DripGame's channels</td></tr><tr><td>level (of a chip)</td><td>a chip's output at the node in mixer units, once per CPU cycle, before it joins its leg</td></tr><tr><td>full scale</td><td>a chip's level with every channel at maximum</td></tr><tr><td>silent level</td><td>a chip's level with every channel silent; a non-zero silent level is a constant offset the high-passes remove</td></tr><tr><td>DAC</td><td>a chip's digital-to-analog output value before its gain</td></tr><tr><td>5B tick</td><td>one step of the Sunsoft 5B, every <code>S5B_CLOCK_DIV</code> (16) CPU cycles</td></tr><tr><td>sequencer tick</td><td>one clock of the MMC5's own 240 Hz sequencer, every <code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code> (7458) CPU cycles</td></tr><tr><td>visit</td><td>one Namco 163 channel update, every 15 CPU cycles</td></tr><tr><td>operator, modulator, carrier</td><td>the VRC7's two FM generators per channel: op 0 is the modulator, op 1 the carrier. These words are used for the VRC7 only</td></tr><tr><td>wave unit, modulation unit</td><td>the Disk System channel's main wavetable stepper and its second, pitch-bending table unit (the Disk System's words for what other sources call carrier and modulator)</td></tr><tr><td>wave entry</td><td>one entry of a wavetable: a 4-bit Namco 163 entry (two per memory byte) or a 6-bit Disk System entry; its index is the entry position</td></tr><tr><td>FM update</td><td>one VRC7 output computation, every 72 board-clock cycles (35, VRC7 \"One FM update\")</td></tr><tr><td>PCM level, PCM interrupt flag</td><td>the MMC5's 8-bit PCM level (<code>$5011</code>) and its own interrupt flag, read at <code>$5010</code> (line <code>IRQ_M5PCM</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>tape value</td><td>one signed value of the Study Box tape's recorded waveform</td></tr><tr><td>ladder</td><td>a table of output weights indexed by a volume or level</td></tr><tr><td>gate (5B)</td><td>the per-channel open/closed decision from tone and noise (35, Sunsoft 5B, Output)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>\n<section class=\"sect nesemu-chapter\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-sect\"><h3 class=\"sect-h\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio\">Expansion audio: VRC6, VRC7, Namco 163, Sunsoft 5B, MMC5 and the Disk System</h3><nav class=\"gbemu-toc\"><b>Sections</b> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s1\">What this chapter covers</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s2\">The shared level assumption</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s3\">MMC5 audio (mapper 5)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s4\">The Sunsoft 5B (mapper 69)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s5\">Konami VRC6 sound (mappers 24 and 26)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s6\">Konami VRC7 FM synthesis (mapper 85)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s7\">Namco 163 sound (mapper 19)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s8\">The Disk System wavetable channel (mapper 20)</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s9\">The final mix: how the expansion levels reach the console output</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s10\">Open questions</a> <a href=\"#nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s11\">Registered choices, collected</a></nav>\n<p>Five switches, each 1 by default, set four chips' levels and three of their behaviours; each also sets the default of a sub-switch. Each switch's rule is stated once, in its chip's section (the \"stated in\" column below). The levels are chosen because the output then matches another emulator's: the check <code>audio-compare-exp</code> (each chip alone, one title per chip, against another emulator's output) passes all six items. Terms are defined in the glossary of <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a>, section 16.</p>\n<p>The level switches and the one level constant they add:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>What it selects</th><th>Basis</th><th>Stated in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NES_D147_FDS</code></td><td>1</td><td>the Disk System's level (<code>FDS_FULL_SCALE</code>) and master volumes 2/2, 2/3, 2/4, 2/5; also the default of <code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code>, the modulation unit</td><td>level: matches another emulator; master volumes and modulation unit: the NESdev wiki</td><td>the Disk System, \"Output\" and \"Clocking\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>FDS_FULL_SCALE</code></td><td>8257</td><td>mixer units at wave 63, volume 32, master 0: 63 × 20 × 32767 / 5000</td><td>matches another emulator. The NESdev wiki's \"roughly 2.4x\" a pulse, 11743, moves the level away from matching another emulator's output</td><td>the Disk System, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_D147_VRC6</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>VRC6_GAIN</code> 492; also the default of <code>NES_D147_VRC6_SAW</code>, the six-add sawtooth</td><td>level: matches another emulator; sawtooth: the NESdev wiki</td><td>the VRC6, \"Clocking\" and \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_D147_5B</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>S5B_GAIN</code> 68</td><td>matches another emulator</td><td>the 5B, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_D147_N163</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>N163_GAIN_NUM</code> 29490; also the default of <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> (the channels' average) and of <code>N163_RAM_READ</code> (<code>$4800</code> reads return the memory)</td><td>level and average: match another emulator; reads: hardware rule</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Ports\", \"Clocking\" and \"Output\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each, set to 0, selects the other behaviour, which the chip's own section states beside the default, and <code>audio-compare-exp</code> then fails that chip's item.</p>\n<p>The sub-switches are <code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code> (default = <code>NES_D147_FDS</code>), <code>NES_D147_VRC6_SAW</code> (default = <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code>), <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> (default = <code>NES_D147_N163</code>) and <code>N163_RAM_READ</code> (default = <code>NES_D147_N163</code>). The shipped default is <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> = 1, the channels' average with no multiplex whine; the multiplexed pin (0) is the other setting, not shipped (see the note in the Namco 163 section).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register details of five chips, each behind its own switch</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Switch</th><th>Default</th><th>What it selects</th><th>Basis</th><th>Stated in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NES_R18_N163_ADDR_SAT</code></td><td>1</td><td>the N163 address port's auto-increment stops at $7F</td><td>hardware rule, hardware measurement</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Ports\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL</code></td><td>1</td><td>N163 submappers 1 and 2 have no mixing resistor: no sound</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL_34</code></td><td>1</td><td>submappers 3 and 4 scaled against 5</td><td>tuned; on: the levels follow the NES 2.0 definition's ranges</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code></td><td>1</td><td>the 5B envelope's 32 steps on one 32-level ladder</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the 5B, \"32 envelope steps and up-counting periods\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_5B_COUNT_UP</code></td><td>1</td><td>the 5B's period counters count up and compare</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the 5B, \"32 envelope steps and up-counting periods\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_VRC7_BUSY</code></td><td>1</td><td>the OPLL drops writes inside its busy time</td><td>hardware rule (window), hardware measurement (drop)</td><td>the VRC7, \"Ports and the mute bit\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_MMC5_INVERT</code></td><td>1</td><td>the MMC5 joins the node inverted</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the MMC5, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_VRC6_INVERT</code></td><td>1</td><td>the VRC6 joins the node inverted</td><td>tuned; 1 by default because the VRC6 item moves closer to matching another emulator (ENV 0.811 → 0.842)</td><td>the VRC6, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code></td><td>1</td><td>the 5B cartridge amplifier's compressor</td><td>tuned; on: the curve follows the published hardware recordings</td><td>the 5B, \"The cartridge amplifier\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_DISABLE</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>$4083</code> bit 6 halts both envelopes and resets their timers</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Disk System, \"Register map\" and \"Clocking\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_RELOAD</code></td><td>1</td><td>a <code>$4080</code>/<code>$4084</code> write reloads the timer with the master factor</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Disk System, \"Register map\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_VOL_LATCH</code></td><td>1</td><td>the output's gain changes only at wave position 0</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Disk System, \"Clocking\" and \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_MOD_POS</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>$4088</code> writes at the modulation unit's own position</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Disk System, \"Register map\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_WAVE_HOLD</code></td><td>1</td><td><code>$4089</code> bit 7 holds the output, the wave unit runs on; reads give the current wave entry</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>the Disk System, \"Register map\" and \"Clocking\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>NES_R18_FDS_LOWPASS</code></td><td>1</td><td>the adapter's ~2 kHz one-pole on the wave channel's leg</td><td>hardware rule (filter), the NESdev wiki (corner)</td><td>the Disk System, \"Output\"; the computation in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 13.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each switch's other setting (0) selects the other behaviour, stated in the chip's own section.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s1\">What this chapter covers</h4>\n<p>Six sound generators that live outside the 2A03: five on cartridges and one in the Disk System's RAM adapter. Each one drives the console's audio output node through the cartridge connector's expansion pin. This chapter gives, for each chip, the register map bit by bit, how it is clocked, its channel units, the exact output computation and gain, its power-on state and its registered choices. Where each chip's level joins the console mixer, and which leg it uses, is stated once, in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 12.3; this chapter's last section gives the levels and the clock order.</p>\n<p>The banking, mirroring and interrupt counters of the same chips are in <a href=\"#nesemu-42-mappers-licensed\">42-MAPPERS-LICENSED.md</a>. The 2A03's own channels, its non-linear mixer and its output filters are in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a>. This chapter covers only sound.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Which mapper numbers carry which chip</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mapper</th><th>Chip</th><th>Channels</th><th>Clocked</th><th>Output joins as (<a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 12.3)</th><th>Basis of the level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>MMC5 audio</td><td>2 pulses + 8-bit PCM level</td><td>every CPU cycle; pulse timers every second cycle; own 240 Hz sequencer</td><td>subtracted through the signed leg, only when the mapper is 5 (added to the unsigned mix with <code>NES_R18_MMC5_INVERT</code> = 0)</td><td>not established</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Namco 163 sound</td><td>1 to 8 wavetable channels, time-shared</td><td>one channel visit per 15 CPU cycles</td><td>added to the unsigned mix</td><td>matches another emulator</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Disk System wavetable</td><td>1 wavetable channel + modulation unit</td><td>every CPU cycle, only while a disk image is loaded</td><td>added through the Disk System leg, low-passed (added to the unsigned mix with <code>NES_R18_FDS_LOWPASS</code> = 0)</td><td>matches another emulator</td></tr><tr><td>24, 26</td><td>Konami VRC6</td><td>2 pulses + 1 sawtooth</td><td>every CPU cycle</td><td>subtracted through the signed leg (added to the unsigned mix with <code>NES_R18_VRC6_INVERT</code> = 0)</td><td>matches another emulator</td></tr><tr><td>69</td><td>Sunsoft 5B (alongside the FME-7)</td><td>3 squares, 1 noise source, 1 envelope</td><td>one 5B tick per <code>S5B_CLOCK_DIV</code> CPU cycles</td><td>added through the 5B leg, compressed (added to the unsigned mix with <code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code> = 0)</td><td>matches another emulator</td></tr><tr><td>85</td><td>Konami VRC7 FM part</td><td>6 two-operator FM channels</td><td>one FM update per 72 cycles of a 3.579545 MHz board clock, counted against the CPU clock</td><td>added to the unsigned mix</td><td>another emulator's source</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Mapper 210 shares the Namco 163's banking code but has <b>no sound</b>. Its writes to <code>$F800</code> and <code>$E000</code> bit 6 do not reach the sound part, <code>$4800-$5FFF</code> is not decoded for it, and the sound clock is never run for it (the Namco 175/340 boards carry no sound part).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">What adjudicates any of this</h5>\n<p>No pinned test cartridge exercises any of these chips. The core counted this: 298 cartridge files in the two pinned corpora, none of them mapper 24, 26 or 85, and the two mapper-69 cartridges test only the FME-7's banking. <b>No board row is decided by expansion audio.</b> The evidence that exists is the gate commands <code>nes-test expaudio &lt;cartridge&gt;</code> in the gate set. Each one drives the chip directly, with no processor running, through the board's own register addresses. It reads the chip's level (<code>nes_exp_level</code>) after every CPU cycle.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Gate id</th><th>Cartridge (named only)</th><th>What it checks</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>expaudio-5b</code></td><td>Gimmick! (J), mapper 69</td><td>Channels A, B and C each move the output when set to level 15, and the output reads 0 when the level is set back to 0. Channel A at period 16, tone only, must cross its mean at 1789773 / (32 × 16) = 3495.65 Hz, within ±(2 + 0.2 %).</td><td>measured: connection in both directions, and pitch against the stated formula</td></tr><tr><td><code>expaudio-vrc6a</code></td><td>Akumajou Densetsu (J), mapper 24</td><td>Pulse 1, pulse 2 and the sawtooth each move the output, and each reads 0 when switched off</td><td>measured: connection only</td></tr><tr><td><code>expaudio-vrc6b</code></td><td>Esper Dream 2 (J), mapper 26</td><td>The same probe, applying mapper 26's A0/A1 swap on the way in. A pass shows that the probe and the core agree. It does <b>not</b> show that 26 is the swapped board.</td><td>measured: connection only</td></tr><tr><td><code>expaudio-n163</code></td><td>Erika to Satoru no Yume Bouken (J), mapper 19</td><td>A 32-entry square on channel 7 at frequency $2000 must give 466.09 Hz. With channels 7 and 6 both on it must give exactly half, 233.05 Hz. Volume 0 must hold the output constant, and <code>$E000</code> bit 6 must make it read 0.</td><td>measured: connection, pitch and the time-sharing halving</td></tr><tr><td><code>expaudio-vrc7</code></td><td>Lagrange Point (J), mapper 85</td><td>Six channels keyed on move the output and keyed off fall constant. A sine-like user patch at f-number $120 must give 436.95 Hz at block 4 and 873.90 Hz at block 5. One full-volume channel must swing between 4.48 and 5.48 × <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code>. A 6 dB decay must take 82.4 ms at rate 6 and 41.2 ms at rate 7, ±20 %, measured <b>at block 1, f-number $1FF, KSR clear</b> (so rks = 0 and rl = 0): a user patch with the carrier's attack rate 15, sustain level 15 and decay rate = release rate = the rate under test, channel volume 0; the time is the first carrier period whose swing is below half the swing of the first full period. The prediction is 2^(18 − rate) FM updates of 72 board-clock cycles each. The half-wave bit must cut the negative half.</td><td>measured: connection and pitch. Level, envelope rate and timbre match another emulator: the two bounds are two other emulators' figures.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each arm has a falsifier that must turn it red: <code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX=0</code>, <code>VRC6_GAIN=0</code>, <code>N163_GAIN_NUM=0</code>, <code>VRC7_GAIN=0</code>, <code>VRC7_CLOCK_HZ=1789773u</code>, <code>VRC7_LEVEL_X1000=1000</code>, <code>VRC7_EG_RATE_SKEW=1</code> and <code>VRC7_NO_RECTIFY=1</code>. The MMC5's audio has a probe command (<code>m5audio</code>) that is <b>not</b> in the gate set. The Disk System's wavetable channel has no gate at all. MMC5's feature recorder (below) is adjudicated by <code>m5probe-gate0</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why</b> (the decay check's block). The 82.4 ms and 41.2 ms hold only for rl = 0. At the pitch check's block 4 the same tables give 54.9 ms and 27.5 ms, so the check names its block.</p></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s2\">The shared level assumption</h4>\n<p>Nothing here measures any cartridge sound chip's absolute output level. So every level constant except the VRC7's comes from <b>one stated assumption</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p>a cartridge channel at full volume reaches the output node at the same amplitude as a 2A03 pulse channel at full volume.</p></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code></td><td>4893</td><td>The mixer-unit level of one 2A03 pulse at volume 15: the core's pulse table entry 15, which is 95.88 / (8128/15 + 100) × 32767. The VRC7 gain is built from this number, and so are the VRC6, 5B and Namco 163 gains when their level switches are 0; at the defaults those three take levels chosen because the output then matches another emulator's. Evaluated in single precision the table entry computes to 4894; <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> is the fixed constant 4893 either way (likewise the triangle/noise/DMC entry 127, about 18,392, computes to 18393).</td><td>not established: a consistency argument, not a measurement. The core's default is 4893. The rival is any measured cartridge-to-console ratio, which would differ per chip and per board.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Mixer units are the units of <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a>'s pulse and triangle/noise/DMC tables, where 32767 means a normalised mixer output of 1.0. Every chip below returns an <b>unsigned</b> level in these units once per CPU cycle.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s3\">MMC5 audio (mapper 5)</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register map ($5000-$5015, written)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$5000</code> (pulse 1), <code>$5004</code> (pulse 2)</td><td>7-6</td><td>duty, 0-3 (selects a row of <code>MMC5_DUTY</code>)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5</td><td>length-counter halt, which is also envelope loop</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4</td><td>constant volume (1) or envelope (0)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>volume when constant; envelope period otherwise</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5001</code>, <code>$5005</code></td><td>all</td><td><b>ignored</b>. The chip has no sweep unit.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5002</code>, <code>$5006</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>timer period, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5003</code>, <code>$5007</code></td><td>7-3</td><td>length index into <code>MMC5_LENGTH</code>. Loaded <b>only if the channel is enabled</b> in <code>$5015</code>.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2-0</td><td>timer period, high 3 bits</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(side effects)</td><td>sets the envelope start flag and resets the duty step to 0. The timer is not reloaded.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5010</code></td><td>0</td><td>PCM mode: 0 = write mode, 1 = <b>read mode</b>: every CPU read of <code>$8000-$BFFF</code> that the board answers (opcode fetches and dummy reads included; firmware or work RAM alike) is taken as a PCM byte, by the rule for <code>$5011</code> below.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>7</td><td>PCM interrupt enable: the interrupt line is pulled while the flag (below) is set and this bit is 1.</td></tr><tr><td>(read)</td><td>7</td><td>the PCM interrupt <b>flag</b>; the read <b>clears</b> it and releases the line. Bits 6-0 are not driven (open bus).</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5011</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>the raw 8-bit PCM level, written.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$5015</code></td><td>0</td><td>enable pulse 1</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>1</td><td>enable pulse 2. Clearing an enable bit sets that channel's length counter to 0.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The PCM byte.</b> A PCM byte b (from a <code>$5011</code> write in write mode, or from a read in read mode) is taken by this decision table:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>mode (<code>$5010</code> bit 0)</th><th>where b comes from</th><th>b</th><th>result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0, write mode</td><td>a <code>$5011</code> write</td><td>not <code>$00</code></td><td>the level becomes b</td></tr><tr><td>0, write mode</td><td>a <code>$5011</code> write</td><td><code>$00</code></td><td><b>the level is unchanged</b> and the PCM interrupt flag is set</td></tr><tr><td>1, read mode</td><td>a CPU read of <code>$8000-$BFFF</code></td><td>not <code>$00</code></td><td>the level becomes b</td></tr><tr><td>1, read mode</td><td>a CPU read of <code>$8000-$BFFF</code></td><td><code>$00</code></td><td>the level is unchanged and the PCM interrupt flag is set</td></tr><tr><td>1, read mode</td><td>a <code>$5011</code> write</td><td>any value, <code>$00</code> included</td><td><b>dropped entirely</b>: the level is unchanged and the flag is not set</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Writes to <code>$5008-$500F</code> and <code>$5012-$5014</code> have no effect on sound.</p>\n<p><b>The write counters.</b> Every write in <code>$5000-$5015</code> increments one counter, the one for (address AND <code>$15</code>); which addresses share a counter, and how the export <code>nes_m5_snd_writes</code> reads them, are stated in <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>. The counters are 0 at power-on.</p>\n<p><b>Read <code>$5015</code>.</b> Bit 0 is 1 if pulse 1's length counter is non-zero, and bit 1 is 1 if pulse 2's is. Bits 2-7 are <b>open bus</b>, including bit 7 (the PCM interrupt flag is read at <code>$5010</code>). (Undriven bits read as open bus everywhere in this core.) No other audio register is readable.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking</h5>\n<p>The MMC5 audio is clocked once per CPU cycle, from the cartridge clock step, and only when the mapper is 5. The 2A03's frame counter does <b>not</b> clock the MMC5: its envelopes and length counters are clocked only by its own sequencer (step 2).</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Pulse timers.</b> A half-rate toggle flips every CPU cycle, and the timers step when it becomes 1: on the first, third, fifth… cycle after power-on. On a step, a timer at 0 reloads from its 11-bit period and advances its duty step (0-7, wrapping). Otherwise the timer counts down by one. One duty step therefore lasts 2 × (P + 1) CPU cycles, so the pitch is CPU / (16 × (P + 1)), the same as a 2A03 pulse. (Not established independently: it follows from \"the pulse channels are the 2A03's circuit\". The core's default is half rate. The rival is a full-rate timer, an octave up.)</li><li><b>The 240 Hz sequencer.</b> A countdown starts at <code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code> and drops by 1 every CPU cycle. When it reaches 0 or below, it reloads to <code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code> and makes one <b>sequencer tick</b>, which clocks <b>both the envelope and the length counter of both pulses</b>. The first sequencer tick falls 7458 cycles after power-on. The count is in CPU cycles on every region, so on a PAL machine the rate is 1662607 / 7458 ≈ 222.9 Hz. (Measured on hardware for the 240 Hz and the every-tick clocking; not established at finer resolution, hence the knob.)</li><li><b>Envelope</b> (on a sequencer tick). If the start flag is set, clear it, set the decay level to 15 and load the divider from the envelope period. Otherwise, if the divider is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload the divider from the period, then either decrement the decay level if it is above 0, or reload it to 15 if the loop/halt bit is set. (The 2A03 envelope circuit.)</li><li><b>Length counter</b> (on a sequencer tick). If it is non-zero and the halt bit is clear, decrement it.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why</b> (step 2). The 2A03 alternates its clocks: its frame counter clocks the envelopes on quarter-frame steps and the length counters on half-frame steps (<a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 10.1). The MMC5's sequencer clocks both on every sequencer tick.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<p>For each pulse:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>If its length counter is 0, it contributes nothing.</li><li>If <code>MMC5_LOW_PERIOD_MUTE</code> is 1 and its period is below 8, it contributes nothing. By default this test is off.</li><li>If <code>MMC5_DUTY[duty][step]</code> is 0, it contributes nothing.</li><li>Otherwise it contributes volume × <code>MMC5_PULSE_GAIN</code>. The volume is the constant volume when bit 4 is set, and the envelope's decay level otherwise.</li></ul>\n<p>The PCM level adds level × <code>MMC5_PCM_GAIN</code>. The result is linear: it is the cartridge's own DAC, not the 2A03's resistor ladder. The maximum is 2 × 15 × 326 + 255 × 72 = 28140.</p>\n<p><b>Polarity (<code>NES_R18_MMC5_INVERT</code> = 1).</b> The MMC5's whole output (both pulses and the PCM level) is <b>subtracted</b> at the node, through the signed leg (<code>xs_accum</code>, the Study Box's; <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 12.3): silence is still 0 and nothing steps at power-on. With 0 it is added to the unsigned mix.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Hardware recordings show the MMC5's audio inverted against the APU's. Without the inversion, an MMC5 pulse doubling an APU pulse adds where the console partly cancels.</p></div>\n<p>Castlevania III (mapper 5) writes <code>$5010</code> and <code>$5012</code> once each and never loads a period, length or volume.</p>\n<p>There is no mute for periods below 8. The 2A03's low-period mute comes from its <em>sweep</em> unit, and the MMC5 pulses have none, so a period below 8 plays ultrasonic. (Taken from another emulator's source, quoting the NESdev wiki: \"Frequency values less than 8 do not silence the MMC5 pulse channels\"; the reason is the missing sweep unit.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Tables</h5>\n<p><code>MMC5_DUTY</code> holds the 2A03's duty patterns, indexed [duty][step] (a second copy of the same 3-bit counter and multiplexer):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Duty</th><th>Steps 0-7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>MMC5_LENGTH</code> holds the 2A03's length table (counts of sequencer ticks), indexed by <code>$5003</code>/<code>$5007</code> bits 7-3 (the same circuit):</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Indices</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>10, 254, 20, 2, 40, 4, 80, 6</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>160, 8, 60, 10, 14, 12, 26, 14</td></tr><tr><td>16-23</td><td>12, 16, 24, 18, 48, 20, 96, 22</td></tr><tr><td>24-31</td><td>192, 24, 72, 26, 16, 28, 32, 30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<p>Every pulse field is 0: duty, step, period, timer, length, halt, enable, start flag, divider, decay, envelope period and constant flag. PCM level, read mode and interrupt enable are all 0; the PCM interrupt flag is clear and its line released; the write counters are 0. The sequencer countdown is 7458 and the half-rate toggle is 0. Both channels start <b>disabled</b>, so a length load before the first <code>$5015</code> write is ignored. (The 2A03's rule for a disabled channel.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Declared absences</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>PCM read mode and the PCM interrupt</b> are implemented above (<code>NES_R18_M5_PCM_READ</code> = 1). (NESdev \"MMC5 audio\": read mode \"intended for making the unrolled loop tighter\"; \"If you try to assign a value of $00, the DAC is not changed; an IRQ is generated instead\"; \"Reading $5010 acknowledges the IRQ and clears this flag\".) The interrupt is its own line, so the scanline interrupt's acknowledge at <code>$5204</code> does not drop it. Without them, a title that plays PCM by reading it is silent, and one that ends a PCM run on <code>$00</code> waits for an interrupt that never comes.</li><li><b>The sweep units.</b> They do not exist on the chip, so this is not a gap.</li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The feature recorder (<code>M5U_*</code>)</h5>\n<p>MMC5 is implemented as a declared subset. Every write to a register group sets a per-group \"used\" flag, which the ABI exposes so that a probe can report whether a cartridge reached for an absent feature (measured: <code>m5probe-gate0</code>, 19 of 19: eighteen one-group synthetic cartridges plus an empty control). The group numbers (<code>M5U_AUDIO</code> = 0 for any write to <code>$5000-$5015</code> through <code>M5U_PCM_READ</code> = 17 for <code>$5010</code> written with bit 0 set, and <code>M5U_FEATURE_COUNT</code> = 18) and when the record is cleared are stated once, in <a href=\"#nesemu-41-mappers-nintendo\">41-MAPPERS-NINTENDO.md</a>, MMC5, \"The feature-use record\".</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Registered choices</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Default</th><th>Rival</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MMC5_PULSE_GAIN</code></td><td>326</td><td>0: pulses off. Any measured level.</td><td>not established: pulse table[15] / 15, so a full MMC5 pulse equals a full 2A03 pulse</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_PCM_GAIN</code></td><td>72</td><td>0: PCM off. Any measured level.</td><td>not established: the triangle/noise/DMC table at index 127 (≈ 18392) / 255, so level 255 equals the 2A03's 7-bit DMC at its maximum</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_LOW_PERIOD_MUTE</code></td><td>0</td><td>1: silence a pulse whose period is below 8</td><td>another emulator's source, quoting the NESdev wiki; hardware rule: no sweep unit, so no mute</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code></td><td>7458 (CPU cycles)</td><td>a finer, non-integer period, or a region-scaled one</td><td>hardware measurement for 240 Hz (1789773 / 240); not established at finer resolution</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s4\">The Sunsoft 5B (mapper 69)</h4>\n<p>A YM2149-family sound part on the same cartridge as the FME-7, reached through a second select/data pair.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Ports</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>CPU address</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$C000-$DFFF</code></td><td>select a sound register: the whole value (<code>NES_R18_FME7_CMD</code> = 1; with 0, bits 3-0). A select of <code>$10</code>-<code>$FF</code> addresses no register and the data write that follows is dropped (the part answers only when its upper address bits are 0, as on the AY-3-8910 family)</td></tr><tr><td><code>$E000-$FFFF</code></td><td>write the selected register (all 8 bits are stored)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The FME-7's own register ports occupy <code>$8000-$BFFF</code>. Everything at <code>$C000</code> and above goes to the 5B.</p>\n<p><b>The write counters</b> (<code>nes_s5b_writes</code>, instrument only; a read of register r returns the counter for r AND 15). A data write that is stored increments the counter of the selected register <code>$0</code>-<code>$F</code>. Select writes are not counted, and a data write dropped because the select is <code>$10</code>-<code>$FF</code> is not counted. The counters are 0 at power-on.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register map (as stored; fields are masked when used)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$0</code>/<code>$1</code></td><td><code>$0</code> bits 7-0, <code>$1</code> bits 3-0</td><td>channel A tone period, 12 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$2</code>/<code>$3</code></td><td>same</td><td>channel B tone period</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4</code>/<code>$5</code></td><td>same</td><td>channel C tone period</td></tr><tr><td><code>$6</code></td><td>4-0</td><td>noise period, 5 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$7</code></td><td>0, 1, 2</td><td><b>disable</b> tone on A, B, C (1 = off)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3, 4, 5</td><td><b>disable</b> noise on A, B, C (1 = off)</td></tr><tr><td><code>$8</code>, <code>$9</code>, <code>$A</code></td><td>4</td><td>channel A, B, C: 1 = level comes from the envelope</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>fixed level 0-15, used when bit 4 is 0</td></tr><tr><td><code>$B</code>/<code>$C</code></td><td>16 bits, <code>$B</code> low</td><td>envelope period E</td></tr><tr><td><code>$D</code></td><td>3</td><td>continue</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td>attack (start rising)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>1</td><td>alternate</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>0</td><td>hold</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>$7</code> is a <b>disable</b> register. Reading it as an enable silences the whole chip. (The YM2149 register map.)</p>\n<p>Writing <code>$D</code> with <b>any</b> value, even the same value again, restarts the envelope: step 0, hold cleared, direction = bit 2, envelope timer 0. (This is how the family's music drivers retrigger notes. If the envelope restarted only on a change, every repeated note after the first would be silent.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking</h5>\n<p>Once per CPU cycle a prescaler counts up. When it reaches <code>S5B_CLOCK_DIV</code> (16) it resets to 0 and the chip takes <b>one 5B tick</b>, so the first 5B tick falls on the 16th CPU cycle. On each 5B tick the steps below run in this order. They are written for <code>NES_R18_5B_COUNT_UP</code> = 0 and <code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 0; the defaults (both 1) change them as \"32 envelope steps and up-counting periods\" below states.</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Tones, A then B then C.</b> Take the 12-bit period, treating 0 as 1. If the channel's timer is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload it with period − <code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code> and toggle the channel's output flip-flop. With the fix on, each half-period lasts period 5B ticks = 16 × P CPU cycles, so f = CPU / (32 × P). (A down-counter whose underflow toggles a flip-flop. This matches the YM2149 datasheet's fMaster / (16 × TP) with the master clock at half the CPU clock. The 16 matches another emulator's output, and two sources agree on it.)</li><li><b>Noise.</b> Take the 5-bit period, treating 0 as 1. If the timer is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload it with period − <code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code> and shift a <b>17-bit</b> linear-feedback register right by one. The new bit 16 is (bit 0 XOR bit 3) of the old value. The noise output is the register's bit 0. (The YM2149's 17-bit generator.)</li><li><b>Envelope timer.</b> With <code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code> = 1, the step interval is 2 × E 5B ticks, or 1 5B tick when E = 0. If the timer is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload it with that interval − 1 and clock the envelope. The timer is 16 bits wide, so the reload is clamped at 65535. For <b>E above 32768 the interval saturates at 65536 5B ticks</b>. One step of this 16-step envelope is 32 × E CPU cycles, and a full sweep is 512 × E, which is the datasheet's fE = fMaster / (256 × E) (the factor matches another emulator's output). With the fix off, the interval is E + 1 5B ticks, with E = 0 treated as 1.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Envelope clock.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the hold flag is set, nothing happens.</li><li>Otherwise add 1 to the step. If the step is now below 16, stop there.</li><li>When the step reaches 16, set it to 0 and read <code>$D</code>:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Continue clear:</b> set hold, set the direction to falling and set the step to 15, so the envelope rests at silence.</li><li><b>Continue set:</b> if alternate is set, flip the direction. Then, if hold is set, set hold and set the step to 15.</li></ul></li></ol>\n<p><b>The envelope level</b> is the step when the direction is rising, and 15 − step when it is falling. (The structure is a hardware rule. Not established at phase edges: no available source states the step on which a phase ends.)</p>\n<p>With <code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 0 this envelope has <b>16 levels at 3 dB</b>, while the chip's has 32 levels at 1.5 dB: the chip's timing at half its resolution. The default, <code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 1, runs the chip's 32 steps (below).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">32 envelope steps and up-counting periods</h5>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 1.</b> The envelope has <b>32</b> steps (<code>S5B_ENV_STEPS</code> = 32; 16 with the switch at 0). In the envelope clock, \"16\" becomes 32 and \"15\" becomes 31; the level is the step rising and 31 − step falling. One step is <b>E 5B ticks</b> (E = 0 runs as 1 5B tick), so a full sweep is still 512 × E CPU cycles. Levels index one 32-entry ladder, <code>S5B_LEVEL32</code>, held in sixteenths of a unit: entry 2v+1 is <code>S5B_LEVEL[v]</code> × 16 exactly, entry 0 and entry 1 are 0, and every even entry from 2 is 4080 × 2^(−(31−i)/4) rounded: 0, 0, 27, 32, 38, 48, 54, 64, 76, 96, 107, 128, 152, 176, 214, 256, 303, 368, 429, 512, 606, 720, 858, 1024, 1213, 1440, 1715, 2048, 2426, 2880, 3431, 4080. A <b>fixed</b> level v reads entry 2v+1 (v = 0 reads 0, silence). The contribution is (<code>S5B_LEVEL32</code>[level] × <code>S5B_GAIN</code>) &gt;&gt; 4, so every fixed level gives the same value as with the switch at 0. (The YM2149 has one 32-level output ladder, 1.5 dB a step; a fixed level drives it at 2v+1. Not established: E = 0, which the 16-step setting runs at half of E = 1.) With 16 steps an envelope fades in 3 dB stairs.</p>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_5B_COUNT_UP</code> = 1</b> (with <code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code> = 1). The tone, noise and envelope timers count <b>up</b>: each 5B tick adds 1, and when the count reaches (≥) the current period (tone and noise with 0 read as 1; envelope interval as above), the count returns to 0 and the flip-flop toggles / the noise register shifts / the envelope clocks. A steady period gives the same P 5B ticks as with down-counters; a period written mid-count takes effect at once, and a shorter period than the count so far fires on the next 5B tick. (The YM2149's counters count up and compare.) With down-counters, a slide or vibrato written mid-period waits out the old count (14,077 CPU cycles instead of 13 in a probe).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The cartridge amplifier</h5>\n<p><b><code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code> = 1 (default, tuned).</b> With the switch on, the 5B's level is not added to the unsigned mix. It is summed apart, averaged per output sample, and then compressed: a one-pole high-pass at <code>S5B_AMP_HP_HZ</code> = 90 Hz, an immediate-attack peak envelope with a <code>S5B_AMP_RELEASE_S</code> = 0.030 s release, and above <code>S5B_AMP_THRESHOLD</code> = 20600 mixer units a gain of (envelope / threshold)^(1/<code>S5B_AMP_RATIO</code> − 1) with <code>S5B_AMP_RATIO</code> = 2.7 on the high-passed part. Under the threshold the leg is exactly the linear one. The exact computation, its order and its worked example are in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 13.1, step 3. With 0 the level is added to the unsigned mix.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Tuned to rainwarrior's clip_5b recordings, NESdev forum t=17745: linear up to and including three channels at volume 14, onset about 1 dB above (three channels at fixed 14 = 36720 peak to peak here, centred peak 18360, + 1 dB = 20600), about 2.7:1, about 30 ms release, immediate attack; the 90 Hz corner is our choice. Gimmick!, the only 5B title, never reaches the threshold in the check's window, so <code>audio-compare-exp</code> does not move. It is on because it follows the published hardware recordings, as the N163 levels follow the NES 2.0 definition.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<p>For each channel A, B and C:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>The gate.</b> The tone is on when its <code>$7</code> bit is 0, and the noise is on when its <code>$7</code> bit is 0. The gate is open when the tone is on and the flip-flop is high, <b>or</b> when the noise is on and the noise output bit is 1 (either or both). If the gate is closed, the channel contributes 0. If <b>both</b> tone and noise are disabled the gate is always closed, and the channel is silent.</li><li><b>The level.</b> Use the envelope level if bit 4 of the channel's level register is set. Otherwise use the fixed level in bits 3-0.</li><li><b>The contribution.</b> Add <code>S5B_LEVEL[level]</code> × <code>S5B_GAIN</code> (with <code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 0). With <code>NES_R18_5B_ENV32</code> = 1 (the default) the contribution is (<code>S5B_LEVEL32</code>[level] × <code>S5B_GAIN</code>) &gt;&gt; 4, which gives the same value for every fixed level.</li></ol>\n<p><code>S5B_GAIN</code> = 68 per unit of the 8-bit ladder with <code>NES_D147_5B</code> = 1. The maximum is 3 × 255 × 68 = 52020 (3 × 255 × 19 = 14535 with <code>NES_D147_5B</code> = 0). The ladder is the same at either setting. (Chosen because the output then matches another emulator's.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The level ladder</h5>\n<p><code>S5B_LEVEL</code> (with <code>S5B_LOG_VOLUME</code> = 1) is a ratio of 1/√2 per step, scaled to 255 at level 15 and rounded, with level 0 as silence. (The family's 4-bit level drives a weighted ladder that halves the power every two steps (3 dB per two steps). Re-derivable with a calculator.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Levels</th><th>Weights</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>0, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 16</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>23, 32, 45, 64, 90, 128, 180, 255</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With <code>S5B_LOG_VOLUME</code> = 0, the table becomes linear: level × 17, so 255 at level 15.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<p>Select 0 and prescaler 0. All 16 registers are 0, and their write counters are 0. This means <code>$7</code> = 0, every source <b>enabled</b>, and all levels 0, so the chip is silent until a level is written. Tone timers and flip-flops are 0, the noise timer is 0, and the noise register is <b>1</b> (zero would never leave zero). Envelope timer, step, hold and direction are all 0. Because the step is 0 and the direction is falling, a channel switched to envelope mode before any <code>$D</code> write starts at level 15 and falls once.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Registered choices</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Default</th><th>Rival</th><th>Basis</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code></td><td>1</td><td>0: tone half-period 16 × (P+1), noise shift every 16 × (N+1), and envelope steps every E+1 5B ticks, so a full sweep is 256 × (E+1) cycles: one step long and twice as fast.</td><td>matches another emulator, plus a hardware rule. Reason: a counter reloaded with N that toggles after reaching 0 runs N+1 5B ticks.</td><td>Rival flattens every tone by 1/P (17 cents at P = 100) and halves every envelope time. <code>expaudio-5b</code>'s pitch line goes red.</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_CLOCK_DIV</code></td><td>16</td><td>8: everything an octave up</td><td>matches another emulator, plus a hardware rule (the datasheet formula with the master clock at CPU/2)</td><td>CPU cycles per 5B tick</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_GAIN</code></td><td>68 (<code>NES_D147_5B</code> = 1); 19 with the switch at 0</td><td>0: the chip off. Any measured level is the other rival.</td><td>68 matches another emulator; 19 is 4893 / 255 = 19.2, the shared level assumption (not established)</td><td>Per unit of <code>S5B_LEVEL</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_LOG_VOLUME</code></td><td>1</td><td>0: the linear table</td><td>hardware rule</td><td>The level ladder's shape</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s5\">Konami VRC6 sound (mappers 24 and 26)</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Address normalisation</h5>\n<p>The VRC6a board (mapper 24) wires CPU A0 and A1 to the chip's register-select inputs in order. The VRC6b board (mapper 26) swaps them. When <code>VRC6_SWAP_26</code> is 1 and the mapper is 26, the core swaps address bits 0 and 1 of every write before decoding. It then decodes on the address masked with $F003. The register numbers below are the normalised ones and apply to both boards.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Default</th><th>Rival</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VRC6_SWAP_26</code></td><td>1</td><td>0: neither board swapped. Mapper 24 as the swapped one is the other rival.</td><td>not established. Reason: which board is swapped is a claim. If it is backwards, the rival build makes the mapper 26 title work and stops the mapper 24 title, so the two cannot both improve. The <code>expaudio-vrc6b</code> gate cannot decide it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register map (sound only)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$9000</code> (pulse 1), <code>$A000</code> (pulse 2)</td><td>7</td><td>mode: 1 = digitised. The duty compare is bypassed and the volume goes straight out.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6-4</td><td>duty, 0-7</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>volume</td></tr><tr><td><code>$9001</code>, <code>$A001</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>period, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$9002</code>, <code>$A002</code></td><td>7</td><td>enable. Writing 0 also resets the duty step to 0.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>period, high 4 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$9003</code></td><td>0</td><td><b>halt</b> all three channels: their timers and steps freeze</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>1</td><td>divide every period by 16 (shift right 4)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td>divide every period by 256 (shift right 8). Takes precedence over bit 1.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$B000</code></td><td>5-0</td><td>sawtooth accumulator rate</td></tr><tr><td><code>$B001</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>sawtooth period, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$B002</code></td><td>7</td><td>sawtooth enable. Writing 0 also resets its step and its accumulator to 0.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>sawtooth period, high 4 bits</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>$9003</code> is stored as a whole byte, and bits 0-2 are used. The VRC6 sound registers are write-only.</p>\n<p><b>The write counters.</b> Each write to one of the ten normalised sound registers (after the mapper-26 swap) increments that register's counter; the index of each register is stated with the export <code>nes_v6_writes</code> in <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>. The counters are 0 at power-on.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking: every CPU cycle</h5>\n<p>The VRC6 interrupt counter steps first in the same per-cycle call; see <a href=\"#nesemu-42-mappers-licensed\">42-MAPPERS-LICENSED.md</a>. If <code>$9003</code> bit 0 is set, nothing else happens this cycle. Otherwise the shift is 8 if bit 2 is set, else 4 if bit 1 is set, else 0.</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>Each pulse, if enabled.</b> If its timer is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload the timer with (12-bit period &gt;&gt; shift) and <b>decrement</b> the 4-bit duty step, wrapping 0 to 15. The step therefore counts 0, 15, 14, … 1, 0. Pitch is CPU / (16 × (P' + 1)), where P' is the shifted period. (The VRC6 register description.)</li><li><b>The sawtooth, if enabled.</b> If its timer is above 0, decrement it. Otherwise reload the timer with (period &gt;&gt; shift), then, with <code>NES_D147_VRC6_SAW</code> = 1 (the default):\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>if the step is even, then at step 12 set the accumulator to 0, and at any other even step (0, 2, … 10) add the rate to the 8-bit accumulator, wrapping modulo 256;</li><li>add 1 to the step, and when it reaches 14 set it to 0. The accumulator is <b>not</b> reset at step 14.</li></ol>\n<p>Six adds per 14-step cycle: the seventh accumulator clock (step 12) resets the accumulator instead (the NESdev wiki, \"VRC6 audio\"). With <code>NES_D147_VRC6_SAW</code> = 0:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>if the step is even (0, 2, 4, … 12), add the rate to the 8-bit accumulator, wrapping modulo 256;</li><li>add 1 to the step, and when the step reaches 14, set both the step and the accumulator to 0.</li></ol>\n<p>So there are seven adds per 14-step cycle. A rate above 42 wraps the accumulator inside one cycle with six adds (6 × 42 = 252), and a rate above 36 with seven adds (7 × 36 = 252). The pitch is CPU / (14 × (P' + 1)). (A staircase, not a ramp.)</p></li></ul>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pulse 1 or 2, enabled, digitised mode set</td><td>volume × <code>VRC6_GAIN</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pulse 1 or 2, enabled, digitised mode clear</td><td>volume × <code>VRC6_GAIN</code> when duty step ≤ duty, else 0. Duty d is high for d + 1 of every 16 steps, so duty 0 is 1/16, not silence.</td></tr><tr><td>Pulse 1 or 2, disabled</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Sawtooth, enabled</td><td>(accumulator &gt;&gt; 3) × <code>VRC6_GAIN</code>: only the top 5 bits reach the output</td></tr><tr><td>Sawtooth, disabled</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The three channels are summed with <b>equal weight per unit</b>. (The chip sums internally into one pin, and 15 + 15 + 31 = 61 fits exactly in six bits.) <code>VRC6_GAIN</code> = 492 per unit of (pulse 1 + pulse 2 + saw &gt;&gt; 3) with <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> = 1; all three at maximum = 61 × 492 = 30012 (61 × 326 = 19886 with <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> = 0). (Chosen because the output then matches another emulator's.)</p>\n<p><b>Polarity (<code>NES_R18_VRC6_INVERT</code> = 1).</b> The VRC6's level is <b>subtracted</b> at the node through the signed leg, as the MMC5's (<a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 12.3). With 0 it is added to the unsigned mix. It is tuned (no capture confirms it) and <b>1 by default</b>, because it moves <code>audio-compare-exp</code>'s VRC6 item closer to matching another emulator (ENV 0.811 → 0.842, BAL 0.70 → 0.65, LEVEL −0.52 → −0.60 dB, SPEC 0.980 → 0.979).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Default</th><th>Rival</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VRC6_GAIN</code></td><td>492 (<code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> = 1); 326 with the switch at 0</td><td>0: the chip off. Any measured level is the other rival.</td><td>492 matches another emulator; 326 is 4893 / 15, so that a full pulse reaches <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> (the shared assumption, not established)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<p>Both pulses have control, period, timer, step and enable all 0. The sawtooth has rate, period, timer, enable, step and accumulator all 0. <code>$9003</code> is 0. The register-write counters are 0. Banking and interrupt power-on are in <a href=\"#nesemu-42-mappers-licensed\">42-MAPPERS-LICENSED.md</a>.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s6\">Konami VRC7 FM synthesis (mapper 85)</h4>\n<p>The VRC7's sound part is a reduced YM2413: six two-operator FM channels, 15 built-in instruments and one user instrument. It has no rhythm section and no test register. The board's banking is in <a href=\"#nesemu-42-mappers-licensed\">42-MAPPERS-LICENSED.md</a>.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Ports and the mute bit</h5>\n<p>The board decodes on the address masked with $F030.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>CPU address</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$9010</code></td><td>register select: the whole byte is stored. The register written is select AND $3F.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$9030</code></td><td>write the selected register. All 64 slots are storable, and only those listed below have an effect.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$E000</code> bit 6</td><td><b>mute and hold</b>. While it is set, <code>$9010</code> and <code>$9030</code> writes are ignored, no FM updates are generated, and the chip's output is 0. Bits 1-0 of the same register are mirroring (see 42-).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Bit 6: taken from another emulator's source, quoting the NESdev wiki.) The sound registers are write-only.</p>\n<p><b>A <code>$9010</code> or <code>$9030</code> write</b> is decided in this order:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>If the mute bit (<code>$E000</code> bit 6) is set, the write is ignored.</li><li><b>Busy time</b> (<code>NES_R18_VRC7_BUSY</code> = 1; <code>VRC7_BUSY_ADDR</code> = 6, <code>VRC7_BUSY_DATA</code> = 42, both in CPU cycles). If the CPU cycle count is below the busy-until mark, the write is dropped and the mark does not move. Otherwise the write is taken and the mark becomes the current cycle count + 6 for a <code>$9010</code> write, + 42 for a <code>$9030</code> write. The mark is 0 at power-on. A write made through the host's <code>nes_poke_cpu</code> (outside the clock) skips this step and leaves the mark alone.</li><li>The write is stored: <code>$9010</code> stores the select; <code>$9030</code> stores the value in register (select AND $3F) and increments that register's <b>write counter</b> (<code>nes_v7_writes</code>, instrument only, 64 counters indexed by register; a read of r returns the counter for r AND $3F).</li></ol>\n<p>Only taken <code>$9030</code> writes are counted: select writes, muted writes and busy-dropped writes are not. Without the busy time, a driver writing faster than the chip has every write honoured.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why</b> (busy time). The YM2413's write timing, 12 and 84 of its 3.58 MHz clocks; measured on hardware: writes too close together are lost.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register map</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reg</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$00</code></td><td>7 AM, 6 PM (vibrato), 5 EG type (1 = sustained), 4 KSR, 3-0 MULT</td><td>user instrument, modulator</td></tr><tr><td><code>$01</code></td><td>same</td><td>user instrument, carrier</td></tr><tr><td><code>$02</code></td><td>7-6 KSL, 5-0 TL</td><td>modulator key-scale level and total level</td></tr><tr><td><code>$03</code></td><td>7-6 carrier KSL, 4 DC (carrier half-wave), 3 DM (modulator half-wave), 2-0 FB</td><td>feedback and rectification</td></tr><tr><td><code>$04</code> / <code>$05</code></td><td>7-4 AR, 3-0 DR</td><td>modulator / carrier attack and decay rate</td></tr><tr><td><code>$06</code> / <code>$07</code></td><td>7-4 SL, 3-0 RR</td><td>modulator / carrier sustain level and release rate</td></tr><tr><td><code>$10-$15</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>channel 0-5 f-number, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$20-$25</code></td><td>0</td><td>f-number bit 8</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-1</td><td>block (octave), 0-7</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>4</td><td>key on</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5</td><td>sustain on</td></tr><tr><td><code>$30-$35</code></td><td>7-4</td><td>instrument: 0 = the user instrument in <code>$00-$07</code>; 1-15 = row instrument − 1 of <code>VRC7_PATCHES</code></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>3-0</td><td>channel volume (attenuation, 3 dB a step)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Bit 5 of <code>$03</code> is unused.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The built-in instruments</h5>\n<p><code>VRC7_PATCHES</code>: 15 rows of 8 bytes in the same layout as <code>$00-$07</code>. (Measured on hardware: an optical readout of a decapped VRC7 die's instrument table, published 2019-03-19; identical in two independently maintained tables in other emulators' source.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Bytes 0-7 (decimal)</th><th>Same in hex</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>3, 33, 5, 6, 232, 129, 66, 39</td><td><code>$03 $21 $05 $06 $E8 $81 $42 $27</code></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>19, 65, 20, 13, 216, 246, 35, 18</td><td><code>$13 $41 $14 $0D $D8 $F6 $23 $12</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>17, 17, 8, 8, 250, 178, 32, 18</td><td><code>$11 $11 $08 $08 $FA $B2 $20 $12</code></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>49, 97, 12, 7, 168, 100, 97, 39</td><td><code>$31 $61 $0C $07 $A8 $64 $61 $27</code></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>50, 33, 30, 6, 225, 118, 1, 40</td><td><code>$32 $21 $1E $06 $E1 $76 $01 $28</code></td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>2, 1, 6, 0, 163, 226, 244, 244</td><td><code>$02 $01 $06 $00 $A3 $E2 $F4 $F4</code></td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>33, 97, 29, 7, 130, 129, 17, 7</td><td><code>$21 $61 $1D $07 $82 $81 $11 $07</code></td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>35, 33, 34, 23, 162, 114, 1, 23</td><td><code>$23 $21 $22 $17 $A2 $72 $01 $17</code></td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>53, 17, 37, 0, 64, 115, 114, 1</td><td><code>$35 $11 $25 $00 $40 $73 $72 $01</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>181, 1, 15, 15, 168, 165, 81, 2</td><td><code>$B5 $01 $0F $0F $A8 $A5 $51 $02</code></td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>23, 193, 36, 7, 248, 248, 34, 18</td><td><code>$17 $C1 $24 $07 $F8 $F8 $22 $12</code></td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>113, 35, 17, 6, 101, 116, 24, 22</td><td><code>$71 $23 $11 $06 $65 $74 $18 $16</code></td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>1, 2, 211, 5, 201, 149, 3, 2</td><td><code>$01 $02 $D3 $05 $C9 $95 $03 $02</code></td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>97, 99, 12, 0, 148, 192, 51, 246</td><td><code>$61 $63 $0C $00 $94 $C0 $33 $F6</code></td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>33, 114, 13, 0, 193, 213, 86, 6</td><td><code>$21 $72 $0D $00 $C1 $D5 $56 $06</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The other tables</h5>\n<p><code>VRC7_MULT2</code> is the frequency multiplier, held <b>doubled</b> so that ×½ is an integer, indexed by MULT. The repeated 20, 24 and 30 are the chip's: ×11, ×13 and ×14 do not exist on it. (The YM2413 datasheet.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>MULT</th><th>Doubled multiplier</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>16, 18, 20, 20, 24, 24, 30, 30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>VRC7_KSL</code> is the key-scale level in 0.75 dB units, indexed by the top four f-number bits (f-number &gt;&gt; 5), at block 7. Its shape is 6 dB per octave of f-number: 18 dB + 6 dB × log2(n) for n ≥ 1, quantised. (The shape is a hardware rule; the quantised values are taken from another emulator's source.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Index</th><th>Units of 0.75 dB</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>0, 24, 32, 37, 40, 43, 45, 47</td></tr><tr><td>8-15</td><td>48, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code> holds the envelope step patterns, one row per rl (0-3), with 8 phases each. A row's ones count 4, 5, 6 and 7 of 8, so rl adds a quarter of a doubling. (Taken from another emulator's source, credited there to published YM2413 hardware measurements.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>rl</th><th>Phases 0-7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>PM_TRI</code> is the vibrato triangle, 8 steps:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>0, 1, 2, 1, 0, -1, -2, -1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The log-sine and exponential tables.</b> These are not transcribed. They are generated at power-on from their formulas, for i = 0 … 255:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>log-sine[i] = round(−log2(sin((i + 0.5) × π / 512)) × 256)</li><li>exp[i] = round((2^(i/256) − 1) × 1024)</li></ul>\n<p>\"Round\" means add 0.5 and truncate. Evaluate in double precision; the core uses series expansions because the wasm build has no maths library, and any accurate evaluation gives the same integers. Check values: log-sine[0..3] = 2137, 1731, 1543, 1419, and log-sine[250..255] are all 0. exp[0..3] = 0, 3, 6, 8, and exp[255] = 1018. (Taken from another emulator's source: the YM2413 family's on-die tables were shown by die readout to be built this way. Adding in the log domain is multiplying in the linear one.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Envelope states and limits</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_DAMP</code></td><td>0</td><td>key-on damp: a fast fall to silence before the attack</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_ATTACK</code></td><td>1</td><td>attack</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_DECAY</code></td><td>2</td><td>decay towards the sustain level</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_SUSTAIN</code></td><td>3</td><td>sustain</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_RELEASE</code></td><td>4</td><td>release, which is also the power-on state</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_SILENT</code></td><td>127</td><td>maximum envelope attenuation: 7 bits at 0.375 dB a step (0 = loudest)</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_FLOOR</code></td><td>123</td><td>the damp phase ends at or above this, and an operator whose envelope is above it outputs 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking</h5>\n<p>Every CPU cycle, a clock accumulator gains <code>VRC7_CLOCK_HZ</code>. When it reaches 72 × the CPU clock, the chip subtracts that amount and generates <b>one FM update</b>; if the mute bit is set the FM update is skipped, though the accumulator still advances. The CPU clock used is 1662607 Hz on PAL and <b>1789773 Hz on every other region, Dendy included</b>. On NTSC this is one FM update every 36.00001 CPU cycles (3579545 / 72 = 49715.9 Hz). On PAL it is one every 33.44 CPU cycles, because the board's crystal does not change with the console. (3.579545 MHz is the NTSC colour clock and the NTSC CPU runs at half of it. The 72 chip clocks per FM update (18 operator slots × 4) are taken from another emulator's source.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">One FM update</h5>\n<p>The steps below run in order once per FM update.</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Add 1 to the 16-bit envelope counter <code>cnt</code>, wrapping at 65536. All of this FM update's tests use the new value.</li><li><b>Tremolo (AM) value.</b> Let a = AM position / 64, so a runs 0-209:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>a</th><th>AM value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>below 104</td><td>a / 8 (0-12)</td></tr><tr><td>104-106</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>107-202</td><td>12 − (a − 107) / 8</td></tr><tr><td>203-209</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The AM position then advances by 1 and wraps at 13440 (210 steps of 64 FM updates), giving about 3.70 Hz. (Taken from another emulator's source; measured by its authors on a real YM2413.)</p></li><li><b>Vibrato step.</b> The step is bits 10-12 of the 16-bit vibrato position, which then advances by 1. One step lasts 1024 FM updates, so the eight-step cycle runs at about 6.07 Hz.</li><li><b>For each channel 0-5</b>, in order. Read the f-number (9 bits), block, key, sustain, instrument and volume. The instrument's 8 bytes are the user registers <code>$00-$07</code> when the instrument is 0, and row instrument − 1 of <code>VRC7_PATCHES</code> otherwise. Byte n is \"p[n]\" below.\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Key edges.</b> If the key is on now and was off at the last FM update, put <b>both</b> operators into DAMP. If the key is off now and was on at the last FM update, put the <b>carrier only</b> into RELEASE. Then remember the key.</li><li><b>Vibrato offset.</b> Let pmk = f-number &gt;&gt; 6, the top three bits. The offset is <code>PM_TRI</code>[step] × pmk, halved with rounding toward zero.</li><li><b>Key-scale base.</b> ksl_base = <code>VRC7_KSL</code>[f-number &gt;&gt; 5] − 8 × (7 − block). This value is signed.</li><li><b>The modulator, then the carrier.</b> For the modulator (op 0) the operator's byte p[op] is p[0]; for the carrier (op 1) it is p[1]. Likewise p[2+op], p[4+op] and p[6+op].\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Key-scale rate.</b> rks = (block × 2 + f-number bit 8) if KSR is set, else block &gt;&gt; 1. Let rl = rks AND 3.</li><li><b>Rate R for the current state:</b>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>R</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>modulator and key off (any state)</td><td>0: frozen</td></tr><tr><td>DAMP</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>ATTACK</td><td>AR</td></tr><tr><td>DECAY</td><td>DR</td></tr><tr><td>SUSTAIN</td><td>0 if EG type is 1 (sustained tone), else RR</td></tr><tr><td>RELEASE</td><td>5 if the channel's sustain bit is on; else RR if EG type is 1; else 7</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li><li><b>Envelope step, only if R is not 0.</b>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>rh = R + (rks &gt;&gt; 2) + <code>VRC7_EG_RATE_SKEW</code>, capped at 15.</li><li><b>The shift.</b> In ATTACK it is 13 − rh when 0 &lt; rh &lt; 12, and 0 otherwise. In every other state it is 13 − rh when rh &lt; 13, and 0 otherwise. mask = 2^shift − 1.</li><li><b>In ATTACK.</b> Act only when the envelope is above 0 and (cnt AND mask AND NOT 3) = 0. Let ph = (cnt AND $C) &gt;&gt; 1, which is 0, 2, 4 or 6. The step size s is:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>rh</th><th>s</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>12-14</td><td>(16 − rh) − <code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code>[rl][ph]</td></tr><tr><td>below 12</td><td>4 if <code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code>[rl][(cnt &gt;&gt; shift) AND 7] is 1, else 0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>If s is not 0, the envelope becomes max(0, eg − (eg &gt;&gt; s) − 1). This makes the attack exponential.</p></li><li><b>In other states.</b> Act only when (cnt AND mask) = 0. Add d to the envelope, capped at 127, where d is:\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>rh</th><th>d</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>15</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code>[rl][(cnt AND $C) &gt;&gt; 1] + 1</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code>[rl][((cnt AND $C) &gt;&gt; 1) OR (cnt AND 1)]</td></tr><tr><td>below 13</td><td><code>VRC7_EG_PAT</code>[rl][(cnt &gt;&gt; shift) AND 7]</td></tr></tbody></table></div></li></ul>\n<p>(Taken from another emulator's source, credited there to published YM2413 measurements. The absolute times are not established by any measurement here.)</p></li><li><b>State transitions</b>, after the step and in the same FM update:\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>DAMP.</b> If the envelope is ≥ 123 and (cnt AND mask) = 0, where mask comes from step 3 (0 when R was 0), let arh = AR + (rks &gt;&gt; 2), with no skew. If arh ≥ 15, go to DECAY with the envelope set to 0. Otherwise go to ATTACK. Either way, reset this operator's 19-bit phase to 0.</li><li><b>ATTACK.</b> When the envelope reaches 0, go to DECAY.</li><li><b>DECAY.</b> When (envelope &gt;&gt; 3) equals SL (p[6+op] &gt;&gt; 4), go to SUSTAIN. SL is in 3 dB units.</li><li>SUSTAIN and RELEASE have no exit.</li></ul></li><li><b>Phase.</b> f2 = f-number × 2, plus the vibrato offset if PM is set, floored at 0. The increment is ((f2 × <code>VRC7_MULT2</code>[MULT]) &lt;&lt; block) &gt;&gt; 2. The phase becomes (phase + increment) AND $7FFFF, a 19-bit accumulator. For MULT 1 this is f = fnum × fs × 2^(block−1) / 2^18. (The datasheet formula written as an accumulator; measured: <code>expaudio-vrc7</code> pitch at two octaves.)</li><li><b>Attenuation.</b> All terms are in 0.375 dB units.\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>KSL term: with kslsel = p[2+op] &gt;&gt; 6, it is (ksl_base × 2) &gt;&gt; (3 − kslsel) when kslsel ≠ 0 and ksl_base &gt; 0, else 0. KSL 3 applies the table in full, 2 applies half and 1 applies a quarter.</li><li>TL term: the carrier uses channel volume × 8 (3 dB a step). The modulator uses (p[2] AND 63) × 2 (0.75 dB a step).</li><li>AM term: the AM value if the AM bit is set, else 0.</li><li>att = envelope + TL term + KSL term + AM term, capped at 127.</li></ul></li><li><b>Operator output.</b>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The index is phase &gt;&gt; 9, 10 bits.</li><li>Rectify: the carrier uses DC (p[3] bit 4) and the modulator uses DM (p[3] bit 3). Both are forced to 0 when <code>VRC7_NO_RECTIFY</code> is 1.</li><li>If the envelope is above 123, the output is 0.</li><li><b>Modulator:</b> if FB = p[3] AND 7 is not 0, add (last output + the output before it) shifted right arithmetically by (9 − FB) to the index. (Averaging two FM updates' outputs stops oscillation at half the FM update rate.)</li><li><b>Carrier:</b> add the modulator's output from this FM update to the index, unscaled.</li><li>Then evaluate the waveform function (below) with that index, the rectify flag and att.</li><li>Shift this operator's output history: the previous output becomes the older one, and this output becomes the latest. This happens even when the output is 0.</li><li>The modulator's output is kept for the carrier. The carrier's output is added to the FM sum.</li></ul></li></ol></li></ol></li><li>The FM sum of six carriers is held as the chip's value until the next FM update.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The waveform function.</b></p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Take the index AND 1023.</li><li>If bit 9 is set and rectify is on, the result is 0.</li><li>Let q = index AND 255. If bit 8 is set, use 255 − q instead (the mirrored quarter-wave).</li><li>Let A = log-sine[q] + 16 × att. If A ≥ 4096, the result is 0.</li><li>Otherwise the magnitude is ((exp[(A AND 255) XOR 255] + 1024) &gt;&gt; (A &gt;&gt; 8)) × 2.</li><li>If bit 9 is set, the result is −magnitude − 1. Otherwise it is +magnitude.</li></ol>\n<p>The range is −4085 … +4084.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<p>When muted the output is 0. Otherwise it is (sum + 6 × 4096) × <code>VRC7_GAIN_NUM</code> / <code>VRC7_GAIN_DEN</code>, computed in 64-bit integers with truncating division.</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><code>VRC7_GAIN_NUM</code> = <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> × <code>VRC7_GAIN</code> × <code>VRC7_LEVEL_X1000</code></li><li><code>VRC7_GAIN_DEN</code> = 8188 × 1000</li></ul>\n<p>At the defaults the silent level is 72769, a constant offset that the output stage's high-pass filters remove. One full-volume carrier swings about ±12100 mixer units, about 4.95 × a full 2A03 pulse. Six channels at full volume together can exceed the 16-bit output and clip after filtering.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>VRC7_GAIN</code></td><td>1</td><td>Scale on the summed carriers. A full carrier swing (taken as 8188 peak to peak) maps to <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> before the level ratio. Rival 0: exact silence.</td><td>not established (a knob)</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_CLOCK_HZ</code></td><td>3579545</td><td>The board's crystal. Rival 1789773 (the CPU clock): every note an octave low, and <code>expaudio-vrc7</code> goes red.</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_LEVEL_X1000</code></td><td>4955</td><td>A full channel's swing as a multiple of a full 2A03 pulse, × 1000. Rival 1000: the shared assumption, which turns <code>expaudio-vrc7</code>'s level line red.</td><td>another emulator's source. Reason: two emulators' mixers put a full VRC7 channel at 4.48 × and 5.48 × a full pulse, 1.7 dB apart. Neither records a measurement, so the core takes the geometric mean, which is within 0.9 dB of both. Why it is large: the VRC7's DAC drives the expansion pin directly.</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_RATE_SKEW</code></td><td>0</td><td>Added to every envelope rate. Rival 1 halves every envelope time. It exists only so that the envelope check can be seen to fail.</td><td>test tools only (a falsifier switch). Every real build uses 0.</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_NO_RECTIFY</code></td><td>0</td><td>Rival 1 ignores the DC/DM half-wave bits. It exists only so that the timbre check can be seen to fail.</td><td>test tools only (a falsifier switch). Every real build uses 0.</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_SILENT</code></td><td>127</td><td>(above)</td><td>another emulator's source</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_FLOOR</code></td><td>123</td><td>(above)</td><td>another emulator's source</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The other fixed levels: one envelope step is 0.375 dB, TL is 0.75 dB a step, channel volume is 3 dB a step, sustain level is 3 dB a step, and AM depth is 13 steps = 4.875 dB (the datasheet says 4.8 dB). (YM2413 datasheet values.)</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>Select is 0, and all 64 registers and their write counters are 0.</li><li>Mute is 0, and the clock accumulator, envelope counter, AM position and vibrato position are all 0; the busy-until mark is 0.</li><li>The sum is 0, and all six remembered keys are off.</li><li>All 12 operators have phase 0, envelope 127, state RELEASE and both history outputs 0.</li><li>The log-sine and exp tables are built.</li></ul>\n<p>The FM part is silent until a key is pressed.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Not implemented</h5>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>The YM2413's rhythm section, which the VRC7 does not have.</li><li>The test register.</li><li>The exact coupling between a carrier's key-on damp and its modulator's. Here each operator damps and resets its own phase independently.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s7\">Namco 163 sound (mapper 19)</h4>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Ports</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>CPU address</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$F800-$FFFF</code> (write)</td><td>bits 6-0: address into the chip's 128-byte memory. Bit 7: auto-increment. Mapper 19 only.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4800-$4FFF</code> (write)</td><td>write the memory at the address. If auto-increment is set, the address then advances (rule below).</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4800-$4FFF</code> (read)</td><td>With <code>N163_RAM_READ</code> = 1 (the default) it returns the memory at the address and, if auto-increment is set, advances the address by the same rule. The read is visible to the program. With 0 it returns <b>0</b> with no side effect.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$E000-$E7FF</code> bit 6</td><td><b>sound off</b>: the clock stops entirely (divider and channel pointer freeze) and the output is 0. The low bits are a program bank (see 42-). Mapper 19 only.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The address advance (<code>NES_R18_N163_ADDR_SAT</code> = 1).</b> After a <code>$4800</code> write or read with auto-increment on, the address becomes address + 1, except that $7F stays $7F. Writes and reads use this same rule. With the switch at 0, $7F wraps to $00.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The port is a memory port both ways (<code>N163_RAM_READ</code>: hardware rule, the hardware returns the memory with auto-increment; taken from another emulator's source). The saturation is measured on hardware: HeeminTV's n163_addresswrappingtest on an 8816 chip. With a wrap instead, a driver that streams past the top of the memory overwrites its wavetables at $00.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">The 128-byte memory</h5>\n<p>One memory holds both the wavetables and the channel registers. Wave entries are 4 bits, two per byte, <b>low nibble first</b>: entry position s is in byte s &gt;&gt; 1, in the high nibble if s is odd. Channel n (0-7) owns bytes $40 + 8n … $47 + 8n, so channel 7 is at the top, <code>$78-$7F</code>. A long wavetable may overlap the registers of channels that are switched off.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Byte</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>+0</td><td>7-0</td><td>frequency, bits 0-7</td></tr><tr><td>+1</td><td>7-0</td><td>phase, bits 0-7</td></tr><tr><td>+2</td><td>7-0</td><td>frequency, bits 8-15</td></tr><tr><td>+3</td><td>7-0</td><td>phase, bits 8-15</td></tr><tr><td>+4</td><td>7-2</td><td>length: 256 − (byte AND $FC) wave entries</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>1-0</td><td>frequency, bits 16-17</td></tr><tr><td>+5</td><td>7-0</td><td>phase, bits 16-23</td></tr><tr><td>+6</td><td>7-0</td><td>wave start address, in wave entries</td></tr><tr><td>+7</td><td>3-0</td><td>volume</td></tr><tr><td><code>$7F</code> only</td><td>6-4</td><td>number of enabled channels − 1 (so 1-8 channels)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Taken from another emulator's source and the NESdev wiki.)</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>A note on the Namco 163's whine.</b> Real hardware multiplexes the chip's sound channels: it outputs one channel at a time, switching every 15 CPU cycles. With many channels active this switching produces an audible high-pitched whine (about 15 kHz with 8 channels), and how much of it reaches the listener depends on the console's output path. <b>The core mixes the channels smoothly (an average) and omits the whine.</b> The multiplexed output is selected by the build switch <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX=0</code>.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking: time-shared, one channel per 15 CPU cycles</h5>\n<p>Every CPU cycle, unless the sound is off, a divider counts up. When it reaches 15 it resets to 0 and the chip <b>visits</b> the current channel c:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Advance the phase.</b> Read the channel's 18-bit frequency and 24-bit phase. The new phase is (phase + frequency) modulo (length × 65536). Write it back into the channel's three phase bytes, so a program can read or reset it.</li><li><b>Read the wave entry.</b> The entry position is ((phase &gt;&gt; 16) + start address) AND 255. Read the 4-bit wave entry e there. The channel's level is L_c = (e − 8) × volume, a signed value from −120 to +105.</li><li><b>Set the DAC.</b> It <b>holds</b> until the next visit.\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>With <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> = 1 (the default), the DAC becomes the <b>average of the enabled channels</b>: let k = ((byte $7F &gt;&gt; 4) AND 7) and lowest = 7 − k (channels lowest … 7 are enabled, k + 1 of them). For each enabled channel j, from 7 down to lowest: if j = c, take L_c from step 2; otherwise recompute j's level from the memory as it stands: base b = $40 + 8j, p = (byte[b+5] + byte[b+6]) AND $FF, e = p odd ? byte[p &gt;&gt; 1] &gt;&gt; 4 : byte[p &gt;&gt; 1] AND $0F, L_j = (e − 8) × (byte[b+7] AND $0F). The DAC becomes (the sum of these k + 1 levels) / (k + 1), C integer division (truncated toward zero). <b>When c is not among the enabled channels</b> (c &lt; lowest, possible after a <code>$7F</code> change), L_c is not in the sum: the sum is over the k + 1 enabled channels only. No state is added (the context size and save-state layout are the same at either setting).</li><li>With <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> = 0, the DAC becomes L_c.</li></ul></li><li><b>Choose the next channel.</b> If c ≤ lowest, or c is out of range, the next is 7. Otherwise it is c − 1. Visits therefore run 7, 6, … lowest, 7, …</li></ol>\n<p><b>Worked example (the average).</b> <code>$7F</code> bits 6-4 = 1 (k = 1, lowest = 6: channels 6 and 7 enabled). The visit is to c = 7 with L_7 = (12 − 8) × 15 = 60, and channel 6's level recomputed from memory is (3 − 8) × 15 = −75. DAC = (60 + (−75)) / 2 = −15 / 2 = −7 (truncated toward zero). If the program then writes <code>$7F</code> bits 6-4 = 0 (lowest = 7) while the next visit is to c = 6: channel 6's phase is advanced and L_6 formed, but the sum holds only channel 7's recomputed level, divided by 1; the next channel is then 7.</p>\n<p>Each added channel lowers every channel's pitch, because each is visited less often. The switching itself is a tone at 1789773 / (15 × channels) Hz. The pitch is f = CPU × F / (15 × channels × length × 65536) (measured: <code>expaudio-n163</code>, 466.09 Hz at one channel, 233.05 Hz at two).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The average is chosen because the output then matches another emulator's; it is the shipped default. With <code>NES_D147_N163_MIX</code> = 0 the output is the most recent channel's level, held, and the switching tone survives the output filters: +9 to +15 dB above 2.3 kHz over the default average, which matches another emulator's output.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<p>When the sound is off, the output is 0. Otherwise it is (DAC + 128) × <code>N163_GAIN_NUM</code> / <code>N163_GAIN_DEN</code>, with integer truncation. With the default gain (29490, <code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 1) that is 1048 at the DAC minimum (−120), <b>16776</b> at DAC 0 (the silent level) and 30538 at the maximum (+105). Integer truncation gives 16776, not the untruncated 16777. With <code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 0 the gain is <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> (4893), giving 173, 2783 and 5066.</p>\n<p><b>The board's mixing resistor (<code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL</code> = 1, <code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL_34</code> = 1).</b> By submapper:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>submapper</th><th>output</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 or 2</td><td>0 (the chip still runs)</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>the level above × 1883 / 4096 (−6.75 dB), computed after the <code>NES_D147_N163</code> gain, with integer truncation</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>the level above × 3161 / 4096 (−2.25 dB), likewise</td></tr><tr><td>0, 5 and any other</td><td>the level above</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With <code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL</code> = 0 submappers 1 and 2 sound; with <code>NES_R18_N163_SUB_LEVEL_34</code> = 0 submappers 3 and 4 keep the level above. The header table gives 13 library files submapper 2, 5 files 3 and 5 files 5 (King of Kings and Erika are 5).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> Silence: NESdev wiki \"NES 2.0 submappers\", mapper 019, \"No expansion sound\". The scales for 3 and 4 are tuned: the mid-points of the stated 11-13 / 16-17 / 18-19.5 dB ranges, anchored at submapper 5 = the default level. They are on because the levels follow the NES 2.0 definition's ranges.</p></div>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>N163_GAIN_NUM</code></td><td>29490 (<code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 1); = <code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> (4893) with the switch at 0</td><td>Gain numerator. It is a knob: 0 silences the chip, which is the falsifier.</td><td>29490 matches another emulator; 4893 is the shared assumption that one channel alone at volume 15 swings the node as far as a full 2A03 pulse (not established). Rival: any measured level.</td></tr><tr><td><code>N163_GAIN_DEN</code></td><td>225</td><td>The width of the DAC range, −120 … +105</td><td>hardware rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>N163_RAM_READ</code></td><td>1 (= <code>NES_D147_N163</code>)</td><td>0: <code>$4800</code> reads return 0 with no side effect</td><td>hardware rule: the hardware returns the memory with auto-increment. With 0 a program that keeps data in the chip's memory reads 0 there.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<p>The memory is all 0 (not established: the hardware's content is unknown). Address 0, auto-increment off, sound on, divider 0, current channel <b>7</b>, DAC 0.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s8\">The Disk System wavetable channel (mapper 20)</h4>\n<p>The RAM adapter's sound channel has a 64-step table of 6-bit wave entries (the <b>wave unit</b>: a frequency accumulator stepping the table), a volume envelope, and a <b>modulation unit</b>: a second 64-entry table whose entries step a running counter that bends the wave unit's pitch. Its registers sit in the adapter's <code>$4020-$4092</code> window, which is decoded only for mapper 20. Drive registers are covered in <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds\">50-FDS.md</a>.</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Register map (sound)</h5>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Bits</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>$4023</code></td><td>1</td><td><b>sound enable</b>. While it is 0 the channel neither clocks nor outputs. Bit 0 is the drive's I/O enable.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4040-$407F</code> (write)</td><td>5-0</td><td>wavetable entry (address AND 63). Taken <b>only while <code>$4089</code> bit 7 is set</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4040-$407F</code> (read)</td><td></td><td>the adapter drives the whole byte: bits 5-0 an entry, bit 6 = 1, bit 7 = 0 (the entry OR $40). Always readable. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_WAVE_HOLD</code> = 1 (the default) the entry is wave[address AND 63] while <code>$4089</code> bit 7 is set and the entry at the current wave position while it is clear. With 0 it is always wave[address AND 63].</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4080</code></td><td>7</td><td>volume envelope disable. When 1, the volume is set directly to bits 5-0.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6</td><td>envelope direction: 1 = increase, 0 = decrease</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5-0</td><td>envelope speed</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(side effect)</td><td>the volume timer is set to 8 × (speed + 1) × <code>$408A</code>, the periodic reload's own expression (<code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_RELOAD</code> = 1); with 0, to (speed + 1) × 8, <b>without</b> the <code>$408A</code> factor</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4082</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>wave frequency, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4083</code></td><td>3-0</td><td>wave frequency, high 4 bits</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>7</td><td>wave halt. Setting it also resets the wave position and accumulator to 0.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6</td><td>envelope disable (<code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_DISABLE</code> = 1): halts both envelopes, and a write with bit 6 set sets both envelope timers to their period, 8 × (speed + 1) × <code>$408A</code>. With 0 it is <b>stored and never used.</b></td></tr><tr><td><code>$4084</code></td><td>7</td><td>sweep (modulation-depth envelope) disable. When 1, the depth is set directly to bits 5-0.</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>6</td><td>sweep direction: 1 = increase</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>5-0</td><td>sweep speed</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>(side effect)</td><td>the sweep timer is set to 8 × (speed + 1) × <code>$408A</code> (<code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_RELOAD</code> = 1); with 0, to (speed + 1) × 8</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4085</code></td><td>6-0</td><td>modulation counter, set directly as a 7-bit signed value: 64-127 mean −64 … −1</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4086</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>modulation frequency, low 8 bits</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4087</code></td><td>3-0</td><td>modulation frequency, high 4 bits</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>7</td><td>modulation halt. Setting it resets the modulation accumulator to 0, but not its position; with <code>NES_R18_FDS_MOD_POS</code> = 1 it also clears bit 0 of the position.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4088</code></td><td>2-0</td><td><b>only while the modulation unit is halted</b>: write the 3-bit value to <b>two</b> consecutive modulation-table entries. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_MOD_POS</code> = 1 (the default) they go at the unit's own position: p = position AND 62, entries p and p + 1, then the position becomes (p + 2) AND 63; the separate write pointer is not used. With 0 they go at a separate write pointer, which advances by 2, wraps at 63 and is never reset except at power-on.</td></tr><tr><td><code>$4089</code></td><td>7</td><td>wavetable write enable. With <code>NES_R18_FDS_WAVE_HOLD</code> = 1 (the default) it holds the output while the position runs on: when a write sets bit 7 while it was clear, the wave entry at the current position is captured, and while bit 7 is set the output uses the captured entry. With 0 it also freezes the wave position (below).</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>1-0</td><td>master volume (index into the master volumes of \"Output\")</td></tr><tr><td><code>$408A</code></td><td>7-0</td><td>envelope speed multiplier for both envelopes. 0 stops both.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Reads of <code>$4080-$408A</code>, <code>$4090</code> and <code>$4092</code> are not decoded and return open bus. With <code>$4023</code> bit 1 clear, writes to <code>$4080-$408A</code> are refused and the wave RAM stays writable (<code>NES_R18_FDS_4023_GATE</code> = 1, <a href=\"#nesemu-50-fds\">50-FDS.md</a>). With 0 the drive's I/O-enable gate (<code>$4023</code> bit 0) blocks only <code>$4022</code>, so the sound registers are always writable on mapper 20.</p>\n<p>With <code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_RELOAD</code> = 1 and <code>$408A</code> = 0 at the write, the timer is 0, and the first step comes on the first cycle the envelope runs (not established on hardware). Without the switch, the first step after every write comes <code>$408A</code> times early (232 × at $E8).</p>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Clocking: every CPU cycle, while a disk image is loaded and sound is enabled</h5>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li><b>Volume envelope.</b> This runs only if all of: envelope disable (<code>$4080</code> bit 7) is 0, wave halt is 0, <code>$408A</code> is non-zero, and (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_DISABLE</code> = 1) <code>$4083</code> bit 6 is 0. Each cycle, decrement the timer. When it reaches 0 or below, reload it to (speed + 1) × 8 × <code>$408A</code>, then step the volume one unit up (to at most 32) or down (to at least 0).</li><li><b>Sweep envelope.</b> The same, with the sweep fields acting on the modulation depth, which also tops out at 32: it runs only if all of: sweep disable (<code>$4084</code> bit 7) is 0, wave halt is 0, <code>$408A</code> is non-zero, and (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_DISABLE</code> = 1) <code>$4083</code> bit 6 is 0. A direct <code>$4084</code> write can set the depth up to 63.</li><li><b>Modulation unit</b> (<code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code> = 1, the default). The counter <code>mod_out</code> is a signed value held in −64 … 63. Only if the modulation unit is not halted and its frequency is non-zero: add the frequency to the accumulator; for each whole 65536, subtract 65536, then read the table entry e at the current position: e = 4 sets the counter to 0; any other e sets counter = ((counter + <code>FDS_MOD_DELTA</code>[e AND 7] + 64) AND 127) − 64; <b>then</b> advance the position by 1, wrapping at 63. The counter is touched only here, once per step.</li><li><b>Wave unit</b> (<code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code> = 1). It runs only if wave halt is 0 (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_WAVE_HOLD</code> = 1, whatever <code>$4089</code> bit 7; with 0, only if <code>$4089</code> bit 7 is also 0). t = counter × depth (signed 32-bit); if (t AND $0F) ≠ 0 and (t AND $800) = 0, add $20; add $400; t = (t &gt;&gt; 4) AND $FF (arithmetic shift); rate = (wave frequency × t) AND $FFFFF; add rate to the wave accumulator; for each whole $400000, subtract $400000 and advance the wave position by 1, wrapping at 63. So t = $40 is unity and the modulation <b>scales</b> the pitch.</li><li><b>The output gain</b> (<code>NES_R18_FDS_VOL_LATCH</code> = 1). At the end of each clock, if the wave position is 0, the output gain is set to the volume. The output uses that gain, not the live volume.</li></ol>\n<p>(The pitch algorithm is the NESdev wiki's, \"FDS audio\"; the order, step then advance, is taken from another emulator's source; stepping every cycle with a 2²² carry is the wiki's every-16-cycles 24-bit accumulator at the same rate.)</p>\n<p><b>With <code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code> = 0</b>, steps 3 and 4 run as follows instead:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\" start=\"3\"><li><b>Modulation unit.</b> Only if it is not halted and its frequency is non-zero:\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>Add the frequency to a 16.16 accumulator. For each whole 65536, subtract 65536 and advance the modulation position by 1, wrapping at 63.</li><li><b>Then, on every CPU cycle on which the unit runs, not only on the cycles where the position advanced:</b> read the table entry at the current position. Entry 4 resets the running total to 0. Any other entry adds <code>FDS_MOD_DELTA</code>[entry] to the running total. The total is a signed 32-bit value with <b>no wrap</b>.</li></ol></li><li><b>Wave unit.</b> The same run condition as above.\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>f = wave frequency + (running total × depth) / 16, truncated toward zero. The modulation term is 0 when the depth is 0. f is floored at 0.</li><li>Add f to a 16.16 accumulator. For each whole 65536, advance the wave position by 1, wrapping at 63.</li></ol>\n<p>The unmodulated pitch is CPU × F / (65536 × 64).</p></li></ol>\n<p><code>FDS_MOD_DELTA</code>, indexed by the 3-bit modulation entry. Entry 4 never reaches the table, because it resets the total instead.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Entry</th><th>Deltas 0-7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-7</td><td>0, 1, 2, 4, 0, -4, -2, -1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Output</h5>\n<p>When sound enable is 0, the output is 0. Otherwise, with <code>NES_D147_FDS</code> = 1 (the default), it is (s AND 63) × min(g, 32) × M[master] × <code>FDS_FULL_SCALE</code> / (63 × 32 × 60), computed in 64-bit and truncated, with M = {60, 40, 30, 24} (2/2, 2/3, 2/4, 2/5 in sixtieths), where:</p>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li>s is the wave entry at the current wave position, or, with <code>NES_R18_FDS_WAVE_HOLD</code> = 1 while <code>$4089</code> bit 7 is set, the captured entry;</li><li>g is the output gain of Clocking step 5 (with <code>NES_R18_FDS_VOL_LATCH</code> = 0, the live volume).</li></ul>\n<p>Full scale 8257 at wave 63, volume 32, master 0. (The level matches another emulator's output; the master ladder is the NESdev wiki's.) With <code>NES_D147_FDS</code> = 0 it is (wave[position] AND 63) × min(volume, 32) × <code>MASTER</code>[master volume] &gt;&gt; 5; the maximum is 63 × 32 × 8 &gt;&gt; 5 = 504 mixer units, about a tenth of a full 2A03 pulse (no derivation of that level is known).</p>\n<p><code>MASTER</code>, the master-volume multipliers in eighths, used with <code>NES_D147_FDS</code> = 0:</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th><code>$4089</code> bits 1-0</th><th>Multipliers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0-3</td><td>8, 6, 4, 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are 1, 3/4, 1/2 and 3/8 (not 2/2, 2/3, 2/4 and 2/6).</p>\n<p><b>The low-pass (<code>NES_R18_FDS_LOWPASS</code> = 1, <code>FDS_LOWPASS_HZ</code> = 2000).</b> The channel's output is not added into the unsigned mix. It is summed apart, averaged over the output divider like the mix, passed through y += k × (x − y) with k = 1 − exp(−2π × 2000 / rate) (rate = the region's CPU clock / divider), and y is added to the averaged mix before the console's own filters. The exact steps are in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 13.1, step 4.</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-key\"><p><b>Why.</b> The RAM adapter's RC output filter; the corner is the NESdev wiki's \"approximated as a 1-pole lowpass with a cutoff of ~2000Hz\", \"FDS audio\". The Disk System level above matches another emulator's output, which has no such filter.</p></div>\n<h5 class=\"gbemu-sub\">Power-on</h5>\n<p>The whole adapter state is zeroed: sound enable, volume, depth, both envelopes, <code>$408A</code>, the modulation table, positions, accumulators, the modulation counter, and master volume 0. The <b>wavetable</b> is set to a square: entries 0-31 = 63 and entries 32-63 = 0.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s9\">The final mix: how the expansion levels reach the console output</h4>\n<p>Once per CPU cycle the audio unit forms one mixer value, in the same units throughout. Which leg each level joins, and whether it is added or subtracted, is stated once, in the table of <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 12.3; the averaging and the 5B and Disk System legs are in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a> 13.1.</p>\n<p>The expansion level comes from one dispatch on the mapper number. It is the VRC6 level for 24 and 26, the 5B level for 69, the VRC7 FM level for 85, the Namco 163 level for 19, and 0 for every other mapper. The matching clock dispatch runs once per CPU cycle in the cartridge clock step:</p>\n<ol class=\"gbemu-steps\"><li>the MMC5 audio clock, if the mapper is 5;</li><li>the expansion clock: the VRC6 for 24/26, the 5B for 69, the VRC7 interrupt counter and then the FM clock for 85, and the N163 for 19;</li><li>the other mappers' counters;</li><li>if a disk image is loaded, the drive, the drive timer and the wavetable channel.</li></ol>\n<p><b>The two instrument calls</b> (<code>nes_exp_level</code>, <code>nes_exp_tick</code>) are defined in <a href=\"#nesemu-70-abi\">70-ABI.md</a>: the level is this dispatch's value, the chip's own output as stated in its section (so it already includes the chip's own mute or sound-off bit, the N163 submapper scaling and the VRC7 gain), and the tick runs step 2 of the clock dispatch once.</p>\n<p>The audio unit then averages the per-cycle mix over the output divider (40 CPU cycles by default, about 44.7 kHz on NTSC). It applies two one-pole high-passes (90 Hz and 440 Hz) and one one-pole low-pass (14 kHz), then clamps to a signed 16-bit sample. All of this is described in <a href=\"#nesemu-30-apu\">30-APU.md</a>.</p>\n<p>The expansion terms are <b>added linearly at the node</b>, after the 2A03's two non-linear groups and never inside either of them. (A cartridge or adapter chip driving the expansion pin is not on the 2A03's resistor ladder.) The constant offsets (VRC7 72769, N163 16776; 2783 with <code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 0) are removed by the high-passes (the output stage's coupling capacitor).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Silent level (mixer units)</th><th>Full-scale level (mixer units)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>one 2A03 pulse, volume 15 (reference)</td><td>0</td><td>≈ 4893 (<code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>MMC5, both pulses + PCM level at maximum</td><td>0</td><td>28140</td></tr><tr><td>VRC6, all three at maximum</td><td>0</td><td>30012 (19886 with <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>5B, three channels at level 15</td><td>0</td><td>52020 (14535 with <code>NES_D147_5B</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>VRC7, one carrier</td><td>72769 (constant)</td><td>swing ≈ ±12100</td></tr><tr><td>Namco 163</td><td>16776 (constant, sound on; 2783 with <code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 0)</td><td>1048 … 30538 (173 … 5066 with the switch at 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Disk System</td><td>0</td><td>8257 (504 with <code>NES_D147_FDS</code> = 0)</td></tr><tr><td>Study Box tape (mapper 186)</td><td>0 (signed, no offset)</td><td>±22528 (a full-scale tape value × 176 / 256)</td></tr><tr><td>DripGame's two PCM channels (mapper 284)</td><td>0 (signed, no offset)</td><td>−7680 … +7620 (each channel (its current byte − 128) × volume, summed, × <code>DG_GAIN_Q8</code> / 256 with <code>DG_GAIN_Q8</code> = 512)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><b>The Study Box tape and DripGame's channels are the SIGNED sources.</b> Each is a waveform centred on zero, so it is summed apart from the unsigned mix (the audio unit's <code>xs_accum</code>) and added to the averaged value at the output divider, before the filters. If you put one in the unsigned sum with an offset, the offset steps the output at power-on, and the source clicks when it is switched on. The Study Box's gain, its timing and its gate are in <a href=\"#nesemu-44-mappers-tail\">44-MAPPERS-TAIL.md</a>, Mapper 186, \"The tape deck\"; DripGame's registers are with mapper 284 in the same chapter.</p>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s10\">Open questions</h4>\n<ul class=\"gbemu-bullets\"><li><b>The levels with the switches at 0.</b> Each chip alone, the output against another emulator's over about 30 s of one title, with <code>NES_D147_FDS</code>, <code>NES_D147_5B</code>, <code>NES_D147_N163</code> and <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> at 0: Disk System <b>−24.2 dB</b> (and +9 to +11 dB in the top two octaves), Sunsoft 5B <b>−10.0 dB</b> (spectrum matches), Namco 163 <b>−13.1 dB</b> plus the switching tone, VRC6 <b>−3.3 dB</b>; MMC5 and VRC7 match another emulator within about 1.5 dB at either setting.</li><li><b>Disk System modulation rate.</b> No test cartridge exercises the wavetable channel. The default follows the NESdev wiki's 7-bit counter stepped once per modulation step, and its output then matches another emulator's; <code>NES_D147_FDS_MOD</code> = 0 steps the running total on every CPU cycle the modulation unit runs, with no wrap.</li><li><b>Disk System <code>$4090</code>/<code>$4092</code></b> (gain readback) are not decoded. The rival is the hardware's documented behaviour, which is to read back the gains. (<code>$4083</code> bit 6 halts both envelopes, as the hardware does, with <code>NES_R18_FDS_ENV_DISABLE</code> = 1.)</li><li><b>Disk System level.</b> 8257 at full scale matches another emulator's output; the NESdev wiki's \"roughly 2.4x\" a pulse (11743) moves it away. The other setting's 504, about 0.1 × a pulse, is not derived from anything.</li><li><b>5B with tone and noise both disabled.</b> The core makes the channel silent. On the YM2149 a channel with both disabled outputs its level constantly, which is how PCM playback through the level register works. The default is silent and the rival is constant level.</li><li><b>5B envelope periods</b> above 32768 saturate. The <b>5B amplifier transfer curve</b> (compression above a threshold in hardware recordings) is tuned and <b>on by default</b> (<code>NES_R18_5B_AMP</code> = 1; the 5B section, \"The cartridge amplifier\").</li><li><b>VRC7 on Dendy</b> uses the NTSC CPU rate (1789773) in its clock ratio, not Dendy's own CPU rate.</li><li><b>VRC7 operator peak</b> is ±4084/−4085 with the generated tables, while <code>VRC7_GAIN_DEN</code> assumes 8188 peak to peak. The level differs from the stated 4.95 × by about 0.2 %.</li><li><b>MMC5 sequencer.</b> <code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code> is an integer approximation, and PAL uses the same count.</li><li><b>MMC5 level and polarity.</b> The MMC5 is not an unsigned term of the mix at the default: it is subtracted through the signed leg, like the VRC6 (the MMC5 section, \"Output\"). Its gains are not established.</li></ul>\n<h4 class=\"gbemu-sub\" id=\"nesemu-35-expansion-audio-s11\">Registered choices, collected</h4>\n<p>Each row's rival and basis are stated in the chip's own table (the \"stated in\" column).</p>\n<div class=\"gbemu-scroll\"><table class=\"gbemu-table\"><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Default</th><th>Stated in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>MMC5_PULSE_GAIN</code></td><td>326</td><td>the MMC5, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_PCM_GAIN</code></td><td>72</td><td>the MMC5, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_LOW_PERIOD_MUTE</code></td><td>0</td><td>the MMC5, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>MMC5_SND_FRAME_CYCLES</code></td><td>7458</td><td>the MMC5, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_PERIOD_FIX</code></td><td>1</td><td>the 5B, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_CLOCK_DIV</code></td><td>16</td><td>the 5B, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_GAIN</code></td><td>68 (19 with <code>NES_D147_5B</code> = 0)</td><td>the 5B, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>S5B_LOG_VOLUME</code></td><td>1</td><td>the 5B, \"Registered choices\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC6_GAIN</code></td><td>492 (326 with <code>NES_D147_VRC6</code> = 0)</td><td>the VRC6, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC6_SWAP_26</code></td><td>1</td><td>the VRC6, \"Address normalisation\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_GAIN</code></td><td>1</td><td>the VRC7, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_CLOCK_HZ</code></td><td>3579545</td><td>the VRC7, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_LEVEL_X1000</code></td><td>4955</td><td>the VRC7, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_EG_RATE_SKEW</code></td><td>0</td><td>the VRC7, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>VRC7_NO_RECTIFY</code></td><td>0</td><td>the VRC7, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>N163_RAM_READ</code></td><td>1</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Output\"</td></tr><tr><td><code>N163_GAIN_NUM</code></td><td>29490 (<code>EXP_FULL_SCALE</code> with <code>NES_D147_N163</code> = 0)</td><td>the Namco 163, \"Output\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>";
function nesApuEmulationTabHTML(d) {
  return '<div class="gbemu nesemu nesemu-apu">' + NES_EMU_APU_HTML + '</div>';
}
